var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#api","page":"API","text":"This page holds the docstrings of the public API of Runtests. using Runtests brings the exported names into scope: @testitem, @testtemplate, runtests, runtestsf, chores, runtests_mono and chores_mono. It also exports Test and the macros of Test, for example @test and @testset. Reach the other public names with the Runtests. prefix, for example Runtests.on_failure.\n\nThe heading of each docstring names the module that defines the name, for example Runtests.Private.runtests. Runtests.Private and Runtests.RuntestsWorkers are internal modules. Always use the public name, for example Runtests.runtests.","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Run-tests","page":"API","text":"","title":"Run tests"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Declare-tests","page":"API","text":"","title":"Declare tests"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Maintain-the-suite","page":"API","text":"","title":"Maintain the suite"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Inside-a-test-item","page":"API","text":"current_testitem() returns a TestItemInfo with the fields name, file, line, attempt and profile.","title":"Inside a test item"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#At-the-REPL","page":"API","text":"","title":"At the REPL"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Run-states","page":"API","text":"","title":"Run states"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Editors","page":"API","text":"","title":"Editors"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/api/#Exceptions","page":"API","text":"","title":"Exceptions"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Runtests","page":"API","text":"Runtests\n\nRun a package's tests as independent test items across worker processes.\n\nRuntests.runtests()                      # every test item under test/\nRuntests.runtests(\"test/solver_test.jl\") # one file\nRuntests.runtests(name=\"adds numbers\")   # one item\nRuntests.runtests(dry_run=true)          # print the plan, run nothing\n\nTest files live under test/ and are named *_test.jl or *_tests.jl. They contain @testitem declarations and nothing else. Shared setup code goes in test/testsetups/ as ordinary modules, which test items load with using. runtests_mono runs several packages of a Julia workspace in one run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Runtests"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.runtests","page":"API","text":"runtests([paths...]; kwargs...)\n\nRun the test items under a package's test/. With no paths, use the package of the active project, or the package whose test/runtests.jl is running under Pkg.test.\n\npaths narrow what runs, while the whole suite is still parsed for errors: a directory, a test file, or file.jl:42 to select the item that line is inside, which is given without other files or directories. Several paths select what any of them does, so the package or its test/ among them is the whole suite.\n\nReturns the run's testset, a RunTestSet named testset_name (\"Runtests\" unless given), holding one testset per test file. Inside an enclosing @testset it is recorded there, so the runs of several calls add up under one, told apart by their names. Outside any, an item that did not pass makes the call throw, which is what fails Pkg.test. A dry run returns nothing.\n\nA process holds one run at a time: a call made while another is running, from another task or thread, throws and leaves that run going.\n\nKeywords\n\nSelection: name (String for an exact match, Regex for a partial one, or a vector or set of exact names), tags (a symbol or vector of symbols an item must carry all of, or a string expression such as \"!slow\" or \"juliac || serializer\"), and group (the name of a tag expression [groups] of test/TestItems.toml declares, or \"all\", the whole suite, or \"default\"). The selections a call gives narrow together. A call that gives none selects the group RUNTESTS_GROUP names, when it is set, and otherwise default: the group of that name when [groups] declares one, and the whole suite when it does not. A name, tag or group the suite does not have is an error that says the nearest it does.\n\nExecution: workers (a count, or 0 to run in this process), threads, timeout, init_timeout and test_end_timeout (a profile's init and test_end expressions are timed separately from the items, and default to timeout), artifacts_timeout (what all the producers of one failed attempt may take together, see on_failure; timeout by default), retries, failfast, memory_threshold, full_stacktraces (keep the framework's own frames in a failing item's stacktrace; trimmed by default), seed (every item draws its random numbers from this and its own name; random unless given, and printed at the start of the run).\n\nOutput: logs (:issues, :batched, :eager, or :quiet, which keeps every item's output in the run's logs and prints none of it), verbose, monitor, monitor_interval, testset_name, coverage (count which lines of src/ and ext/ the workers run, merged into lcov.info at the package's root; also RUNTESTS_COVERAGE, which a keyword overrides and which overrides the file).\n\nState: dry_run prints the plan and runs nothing. replay names a run state (one downloaded from CI, say) and runs it again: the same items, settings, profiles, [order] and seeds, on workers given the Julia flags it recorded, with any keyword given here winning, and a warning naming each package whose version differs from the one recorded and each recorded item the suite no longer has.\n\nEach item: on_item, a function this process calls with an ItemEvent as each attempt at an item starts, and again as it ends with what the attempt cost: its time, CPU time, compilation, garbage collection, allocations and the memory of its process. The calls come one at a time from the run's own tasks, which wait for each, so a slow function slows the run. A call that throws is reported, the function is not called again, and the tests carry on.\n\nThe execution and output settings can also be set under [run] in test/TestItems.toml, which also declares profiles, groups and forced ordering, and by a variable RUNTESTS_<SETTING> (RUNTESTS_WORKERS, say), which the run's header names: a keyword wins over the variable, and the variable over the file. config names another file to read in its place, relative to the current directory, which has to exist; nothing but this keyword makes a run read one. A mistake in the call, such as a misspelled keyword or a value out of range, is an ArgumentError; one in a file or an environment variable is a ConfigError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.runtests"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.runtestsf","page":"API","text":"runtestsf(paths...; kwargs...)\n\nRun the items whose latest verdict was not a pass. A skip does not replace an earlier verdict, and a run stopped before reaching an item leaves its verdict unchanged. Rerunning some failures does not forget the others. Only items still in the suite run; one renamed or deleted since it failed has nothing to run. With replay, the verdicts come from that run and runs started after it.\n\nEvery other selection narrows the failing items: name keeps those it matches, as paths, a file.jl:line, tags and group do, so nothing that is not failing runs. Each item runs with the seed it failed with, so it draws the random numbers it failed with, unless seed is given.\n\nWhen no item is failing, it says so and returns nothing: running the failures until there are none is what it is for. A name that none of the failing items matches is an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.runtestsf"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.runtests_mono","page":"API","text":"runtests_mono([paths...]; packages, kwargs...)\n\nRun the test suites of several packages of one Julia workspace in one run: one plan, one set of workers and one report. Items of different packages share workers when their profiles start workers the same way. Julia 1.12 or later.\n\nThe packages are the workspace's members that have a suite, or those that packages of a [monorepo] table in test/TestItems.toml of the workspace root lists, by directory or glob, and isolated there lists those that run in their own test environment, on workers of their own. The keyword packages narrows them by name or directory relative to the workspace root. Each package's test project has to be a member of the workspace. With no paths, use the workspace of the active project, or of test/runtests.jl under Pkg.test. A path selects within the package that holds it; the workspace root, a package's root or its test/ selects all of it.\n\nEach package's items run under its own profiles, setups and settings. The settings of the run itself (workers, memory_threshold, logs, verbose, monitor, monitor_interval, full_stacktraces, full_names, testset_name, coverage, seed, failfast) come from the call, the RUNTESTS_ variables and [run] of the workspace root's test/TestItems.toml; one in another package's [run] has no effect, and the run says so. The others are each package's own: a keyword or a variable gives every package its value. name, tags and group select within each package; a group or tag only some packages have is no error. config is an error.\n\nBefore any worker starts, a using or import written in an item, a setup or a profile's init or test_end that the package's own test environment could not load stops the run, as do two packages' setups of one name. Each package gets a run state of its own, which its runtestsf, debug and next runs read as one of its own runs. Returns a RunTestSet with a testset for each package, and throws as runtests does when an item did not pass. workers = 0 can run one selected package only, because this process holds one test environment. A dry run prints the combined plan and returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.runtests_mono"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.RunTestSet","page":"API","text":"RunTestSet\n\nWhat runtests and runtests_mono return: a testset that prints as one line. A one-package run has a testset per file; a combined run has a testset per participating package, with that package's files beneath it. RunTestSet forwards other testset operations to its Test.DefaultTestSet, ts.testset, whose fields also read through it. Recorded into a parent or finished inside one, it is the DefaultTestSet that goes into the parent, so no testset tree holds anything else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.RunTestSet"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.ItemEvent","page":"API","text":"ItemEvent\n\nThe event passed to on_item by runtests or runtests_mono, in this process: once as an attempt at a test item starts, kind = :started, and once as it ends, kind = :finished. A retry is another attempt, with a call of each.\n\nIt says which item it is (name, file, line, attempt, and the package and root of its suite), where it ran (worker, numbered as the run's lines number them, w1, and pid, its process), when it started or ended (time, as time() gives it), and how it went (state: :running as it starts, and then :passed, :failed, :errored, :timedout, :skipped, :broken_chain or :cancelled). pid is 0 when no process ran the attempt: the run could not start a worker for it, or it is an item of a chain whose worker died before it ran, which has worker 0 as well.\n\nAs it ends, it says what the attempt cost: elapsed, cpu (user and system time), compile, recompile (of compile) and gc in seconds, allocated in bytes, allocations, how many there were, and maxrss, the largest its process has been resident so far, in bytes. Every figure but elapsed is the whole process's, which runs one item at a time: its worker's, or with workers = 0 this process's. measured says the item measured them itself. It is false as an attempt starts, and as it ends when the run decided the outcome, as for a timeout, a lost worker, a chain cut short or a stopped run: the figures are then 0, and elapsed is how long the attempt held its worker.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.ItemEvent"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.@testitem","page":"API","text":"@testitem \"name\" [kwargs...] begin\n    # tests\nend\n\nDeclare an independently runnable group of tests.\n\nRuntests.runtests() and Runtests.runtests_mono() find test items by parsing test files and run each body in a fresh module, on a worker or in the calling process under workers = 0. Evaluating the macro itself — pasting an item into the REPL, or includeing its file — runs that one item in this session, or on a worker of its own when sandbox, a named profile or an environment directory requires one.\n\nThe body runs at the top level of its module with the REPL's soft scope, as in ReTestItems, so its variables are untyped globals. That matters to a test that measures allocations. Julia measures a plain call such as @allocated f(x, y), whose function and arguments are names or literals, from inside f. Anything else, such as Mod.f(x), a keyword argument or a begin block, it measures where it stands, which then includes reaching the globals. Put such a measurement in a let block, where the variables are local:\n\n@testitem \"summing allocates nothing\" begin\n    let\n        x = rand(10)\n        @test @allocated(sum(x; init = 0.0)) == 0\n    end\nend\n\nKeyword arguments must be literals, except skip, which may be any expression and is evaluated on the worker before the body, with Test and the package in scope. It must give a Bool, and an error in it is the item's error.\n\nKeyword Meaning\ntags=[:a, :b] tags used for filtering\ntimeout=N seconds before a worker attempt is stopped; overrides the run default. An item in the caller's process under workers = 0 has no enforceable timeout.\nretries=N overrides the run default; a chain retries from its first item\nskip=expr Bool, or an expression evaluated on the worker before the body\nfailfast=true stop this item at its first failure\nchain=:sym items sharing a chain run in sequence on one worker\nsandbox=true run alone in a process that is torn down afterwards\nprofile=:name run on the workers of [profiles.name] of TestItems.toml\n\nsandbox and profile combine: sandbox=true profile=:name runs alone in a process of that profile. Neither chooses the item's environment. An item runs in the environment of the innermost directory between its file and test/ with a Project.toml or JuliaProject.toml of its own, and otherwise in the test environment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.@testitem"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.@testtemplate","page":"API","text":"@testtemplate \"name $x\" [kwargs...] for x in xs\n    # tests, with `$x` where the value of `x` goes\nend\n\nDeclare a test item per iteration of the loop, in a file under test/testtemplates/ ending in _test_template.jl or _tests_template.jl. chores or chores_mono runs it in a process with the test environment and the setups, and expands it into the test file of its name without _template, in test/.\n\n# test/testtemplates/periods_tests_template.jl, expanded into test/periods_tests.jl\n@testtemplate \"doubling a $P of $n\" for P in (Dates.Day, Dates.Month), n in 1:2\n    using Dates\n    @test $P($n) + $P($n) == $P(2 * $n)\nend\n\nThe loop runs as Julia's does, in a module holding Test, the package and each package the body imports, by name and nothing more: it says Dates.Day where the item, after its using Dates, says Day. The name interpolates each iteration's values, printed as the item would. A template holds @testtemplates and nothing else: an ordinary @testitem goes in a test file, and a list several templates share in a setup module. In the keywords and the body, $x puts the value of the loop variable x there, written as code, as Threads.@spawn takes $x: repr(x) as the item's module sees it with the body's using and import statements, which has to evaluate back to an equal value there. Nothing binds x in the item, so x written without its $ is refused unless the body binds it itself, as x = $x does.\n\nIn the keywords, a $(...) is computed as the template expands, once per iteration among the same names, and its value written the same way: skip = $(x in MySetup.KNOWN_BAD) is skip = true or skip = false in each item. Outside a $(...) a keyword is the item's, evaluated as it runs. In the body, a $ before anything but a loop variable is left as it is, for a macro of the body's own, and so is every $ inside a quoted expression.\n\nEvaluated anywhere but in a template expanded by chores, it throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.@testtemplate"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.chores","page":"API","text":"chores([path]; dry_run = false, config = nothing)\n\nLook after a package's suite: do what needs no person, then say what does.\n\ntest items and test/TestItems.toml: everything a run would refuse to start on, such as a syntax error, a name declared twice, a setting it does not accept, an [order] entry naming no item or an unknown profile. Reported, never changed.\nconfig files given as config, a path or several, relative to the current directory: each checked as a run given it would check it, as well as test/TestItems.toml.\ntest setups: what setups_to_packages would do, making packages of the setups that are not and adding to their [deps] what they have come to import.\ntest templates: each *_test_template.jl or *_tests_template.jl under test/testtemplates/ expanded into a test file in test/, in a process with the test environment. An expansion whose template is gone is deleted. Every template is expanded because its computed inputs can change without a source edit; a dry run checks expansion stamps and runs no templates. An expansion edited by hand, or a test file in a template's way that chores did not write, is left to a person.\nrun states: this machine's are pruned as a run prunes them, the same ones and no others (see prunable_runstates), with their logs. One recorded elsewhere, a CI artifact say, and one that cannot be read are never deleted.\ncheckouts that are gone: when the project keeps its run states in the default location, the run states there of every checkout that no longer exists, as sweep_runstate_dirs finds them, the directories that held them with them. Nothing else deletes them: a run reads its own directory and no other.\n\nReturns whether nothing is left to do. Having done the rest, it throws a ChoresError when something is left that a person has to fix, and otherwise returns true; so Runtests.chores(); Runtests.runtests() stops before any test runs on a suite a person has to fix first. With dry_run = true it changes nothing and does not throw ChoresError for reported problems; it returns whether there is nothing to do, neither for a person nor for it. An invalid path can still throw. path finds the package as it does for runtests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.chores"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.chores_mono","page":"API","text":"chores_mono([root]; dry_run = false)\n\nchores for each package of a combined run, as runtests_mono finds them, and what a combined run needs on top, in one report with paths from the workspace root. Before any package's chores change a file, it stops for what would stop a combined run and what no chore fixes: duplicate participating package names, setup names or a setup shadowed by a shared dependency, one dependency name declared with two UUIDs, and two packages that set one preference differently. Then it does each package's chores in turn, and the run states of checkouts that are gone once. After them, the report says which items can share a worker: the groups of profiles whose workers are alike, a profile that the workspace shares (monorepo_default) with the packages that use it, and each package's own. It checks each using and import written in the items, setups and profiles of each package that shares workers against that package's own test environment, once the setups' packages have their new dependencies. And it says of a package whose test/runtests.jl does more than call runtests that a combined run does not run that file.\n\nReturns and throws as chores does. A ChoresError names the packages whose chores already ran, and so may have changed files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.chores_mono"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.setups_to_packages","page":"API","text":"setups_to_packages([path])\n\nMake every module in the package's test/testsetups/ a package of its own, so that its precompile cache is kept per checkout and per set of Julia flags instead of in one file that each of them rewrites. Name.jl moves to Name/src/Name.jl, beside a Name/Project.toml that gives it a UUID and lists the packages it imports as its [deps]. Test items load it with using Name, as before.\n\nA package can import only what its [deps] list, so run this again after a setup starts importing something new: it adds what is missing, changes nothing else, and keeps the UUIDs. An import it cannot see, such as one in code that the setup generates or evaluates as it loads, goes into [deps] by hand.\n\nIn a moved file, @__DIR__ becomes pkgdir(MyPkg, \"test\", \"testsetups\"), for the package under test, which names the directory the file was in from anywhere, the REPL included; joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\") becomes pkgdir(MyPkg, \"test\", \"data\"). A setup that does not import the package gets an import MyPkg for it. Nothing is written unless every setup can be converted: an import that neither the test environment nor the standard library provides, a relative include and @__FILE__ are reported instead, all of them at once.\n\npath finds the package as it does for runtests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.setups_to_packages"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.RuntestsWorkers.current_testitem","page":"API","text":"current_testitem() -> Union{Nothing,TestItemInfo}\n\nThe test item this task is running inside, or nothing.\n\nThe value is dynamically scoped, so tasks spawned by the test item see it and a task created before the item started — a long-lived service started in a profile's init expression, say — does not. Neither does a subprocess.\n\nThis is a hook for test infrastructure: temporary directories, fixture paths, switching off telemetry. Library code that changes what it does because it detects that it is under test stops testing the library.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.RuntestsWorkers.current_testitem"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.RuntestsWorkers.on_failure","page":"API","text":"on_failure(f) -> nothing\n\nRegister f to be called with a directory of its own if this attempt at the test item fails, and not otherwise. Producers are called once each, in the order registered, after the item and its profile's test_end have finished and the verdict is recorded, so they see the values they refer to as they are then. What they write is kept with the attempt's evidence; what they throw, or a @test in them that fails, is reported there and never changes the verdict.\n\nOnly in the body of a running test item, or in a task it starts and waits for. Other calls, including one after the body returns, throw ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.RuntestsWorkers.on_failure"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.RuntestsWorkers.artifacts_dir","page":"API","text":"artifacts_dir() -> String\n\nThe directory this attempt at the test item keeps outputs in, made the first time it is asked for. What is written there is deleted if the attempt passes and kept if it fails. The same directory every time within one attempt; another for each attempt.\n\nOnly in the body of a running test item, or in a task it starts and waits for. Other calls, including one after the body returns, throw ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.RuntestsWorkers.artifacts_dir"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.RuntestsWorkers.TestItemInfo","page":"API","text":"TestItemInfo\n\nWhat Runtests.current_testitem() returns inside a running test item.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.RuntestsWorkers.TestItemInfo"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.activate","page":"API","text":"activate([paths...]) -> String\n\nMake this session look the way a test item's worker does: the package's test environment active and test/testsetups/ on LOAD_PATH, so using MySetup and test-only dependencies work at the REPL. Returns the environment's path; deactivate undoes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.activate"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.activate_mono","page":"API","text":"activate_mono([root]) -> String\n\nactivate for the packages of a workspace, as runtests_mono runs them: the union environment of their test dependencies active, and the setups of each package that shares workers on LOAD_PATH, so that the REPL loads what a shared worker loads. With no root, it finds the workspace of the active project (or of the current test/runtests.jl under Pkg.test). An isolated package is not part of it; activate it on its own. A test item runs as an item of the package whose directory holds its file, as on a shared worker: in the union environment, with its package loaded, in its package's root. For an item pasted at the REPL, that is the package whose directory holds the REPL's current directory. Two setups of one name stop it before anything changes. deactivate undoes it. Returns the active project.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.activate_mono"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.deactivate","page":"API","text":"deactivate()\n\nUndo activate or activate_mono: restore the environment that was active and take the setups back off LOAD_PATH. Does nothing if Runtests is not active.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.deactivate"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.is_activated","page":"API","text":"is_activated() -> Bool\n\nWhether activate or activate_mono is in force.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.is_activated"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.debug","page":"API","text":"debug([item]; seed) -> Test.AbstractTestSet\n\nStep into one test item with Debugger.jl: using Debugger first. item picks it: its exact name; a Regex that matches the name of that item and no other; or a path, as runtests takes one, relative to the current directory: file.jl:line for the item that line is inside, or a test file or directory that holds that item and no other. What picks no item, or several, is an error that says what it found. Without item it is the most recent failure among the items that are failing (see runtestsf): of those that failed, errored or timed out, the one whose failure was recorded last, with the seed it failed with, so it draws the random numbers it failed with. When no run of the project is recorded, or no item is failing, there is nothing to step into, and it says so.\n\nThe item runs here, in this process, as a run would run it: its module and imports, the test environment, or the environment of the directory its file is in, and the setups, and its profile's env, init and test_end. Its body is a function the debugger enters at the body's first call. What cannot be part of a function, using and struct among it, has run by then. Only this item runs: the items before it in a chain do not.\n\nWhat this process cannot give the item is listed before it starts: a profile's julia_args, threads and preferences, a process of its own, and the keywords only a scheduler honours. seed is a run's, as runtests takes and prints it, so the item draws the random numbers it drew in that run.\n\nReturns the item's testset. Leaving the debugger before the item has finished records an error, since the rest of it did not run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.debug"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.read_run_state","page":"API","text":"read_run_state(path) -> Union{Nothing,RunStateRecord}\n\nRead a run state: a run's directory, <run>.rundata, or the run.runstate file in it. A file that is truncated, from a crashed run, or written by a different version gives as much as can be read, or nothing. The reader suppresses ordinary read and parse errors so a broken old record does not stop a new run. An interrupt during parsing is rethrown; a file removed between reading and checking its modification time can cause a file-system error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.read_run_state"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.serve","page":"API","text":"serve(path = \".\"; config = nothing, input = stdin, output = stdout)\n\nDrive the test suite of the package at path from an editor: read commands from input and write events to output, one JSON object per line, until input ends or a shutdown command arrives; a run still going is cancelled first. The commands are list, run, cancel and shutdown: each an object naming its command, with an id that the events answering it carry, and for run the items to run, each a package and a name, or their names, and the options to run them with (RUN_OPTIONS). config names a file every listing and run of the session reads in place of test/TestItems.toml, relative to the current directory.\n\nServing over this process's own stdout takes it over for the rest of the process: everything else written there, by the run, by Pkg or by C libraries, goes to stderr from then on, so that the stream holds events and nothing else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.serve"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.serve_mono","page":"API","text":"serve_mono(root = nothing; input = stdin, output = stdout)\n\nserve for the packages of a workspace, as runtests_mono runs them: the listing has the items of every package, each with its package, and a run is a combined run. A run selects items by keys of a package and a name, not by name alone. Each package's own configuration is read; serve_mono has no config argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.serve_mono"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.ScanFailure","page":"API","text":"ScanFailure\n\nThrown when test files cannot be read as a suite: a syntax error, a name declared twice, a Julia file that is not a test file. errors holds every problem found, each with its file, line and msg, so one run reports them all.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.ScanFailure"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.NoTestsError","page":"API","text":"NoTestsError\n\nThrown when there is nothing to run: no test files or no item that a selection matched. runtestsf() with no failing items and no selection says so and returns nothing. msg describes the empty selection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.NoTestsError"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.ConfigError","page":"API","text":"ConfigError\n\nThrown when a run cannot be set up as asked: a TestItems.toml or environment setting it does not accept, a profile or run state it cannot use, or a test setup that does not precompile. msg says what and where.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.ConfigError"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/api/#Runtests.Private.ChoresError","page":"API","text":"ChoresError\n\nThrown by chores or chores_mono when maintenance stops with a problem that needs a person. msg describes the problem; after chores_mono has worked on several packages, it can also name packages whose chores already ran.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Runtests.Private.ChoresError"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coverage/#coverage","page":"Measure coverage","text":"Runtests can count which lines of package source the tests run. It can merge worker counts into one lcov.info file, or Julia can write .cov files under Pkg.test(coverage = true).","title":"Measure coverage"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coverage/#Merge-the-counts-into-lcov.info","page":"Measure coverage","text":"Set coverage = true in one of these ways:\n\nas a keyword: Runtests.runtests(coverage = true),\nas an environment variable: RUNTESTS_COVERAGE=true,\nunder [run] in test/TestItems.toml.\n\nA keyword replaces the variable, and the variable replaces the file. The opening block of the run says which source set the value.\n\nEach worker then counts which lines of src/ and ext/ of the package run. At the end, a one-package run merges the counts into lcov.info at the package root. A combined run includes each participating package's src/ and ext/ and writes one lcov.info at the workspace root. Codecov and Coveralls read this file.\n\nThe paths in lcov.info are relative to the root of the package's git checkout, because these services resolve paths from there. Thus a package in a subdirectory has its directory in front of its paths, for example lib/Foo/src/Foo.jl. Outside a git checkout, paths are relative to the package in a one-package run, and to the workspace root in a combined run.\n\nEvery line of a function that never ran counts as not covered. This also applies to a file that was never loaded. The closing block of the run gives the fraction of lines that ran:\n\n│ coverage: 33.3% of 6 lines in 2 files · lcov.info","title":"Merge the counts into lcov.info"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coverage/#Limits","page":"Measure coverage","text":"Coverage needs workers, so workers = 0 with coverage = true is an error. A worker writes its counts when it exits, also after a timeout. A worker that stops without an exit loses its counts. Examples are a worker that the system killed and a worker that crashed. The closing block says how many workers lost their counts. On Windows, a worker that timed out is also stopped without an exit.\n\nIf the run cannot write the report, the closing block says so. The result of the run does not change.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coverage/#Use-Pkg.test(coverage-true)","page":"Measure coverage","text":"Under Pkg.test(coverage = true), the workers take the coverage flags of Julia from the test process. They write .cov files next to the source files, as the test process does. julia-actions/julia-processcoverage merges these files. You do not have to set anything. julia-actions/julia-runtest uses this way by default.","title":"Use Pkg.test(coverage = true)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/coverage/#Upload-lcov.info-from-CI","page":"Measure coverage","text":"To upload the file that Runtests merges, set RUNTESTS_COVERAGE in the test step:\n\n- uses: julia-actions/julia-runtest@v1\n  env:\n    RUNTESTS_COVERAGE: true\n- uses: codecov/codecov-action@v5\n  with:\n    files: lcov.info","title":"Upload lcov.info from CI"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/profiles/#profiles","page":"Profiles and environments","text":"Three things decide where a test item runs, and each has its own control:\n\nThe profile decides the configuration of the worker: Julia flags, threads, environment variables, Julia expressions to run and preferences. An item chooses it with profile = :name.\nsandbox = true decides that the item runs alone, in a new process.\nThe location of the test file decides the environment: the packages that the item can load.\n\nA profile is a named worker configuration in test/TestItems.toml. An environment directory is a directory under test/ with a Project.toml of its own. The three controls combine freely.","title":"Profiles and environments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/profiles/#Declare-a-profile","page":"Profiles and environments","text":"Profiles go under [profiles] in test/TestItems.toml:\n\n[profiles.bounds]\njulia_args = [\"--check-bounds=yes\"]\nthreads = \"4\"\nenv = { JULIA_DEBUG = \"Main\" }\ninit = \"using MyPkg\"\ntest_end = \"GC.gc(true)\"\npreferences = \"prefs/bounds.toml\"\n\nAn item runs under this profile with profile = :bounds:\n\n@testitem \"indexes stay in bounds\" profile=:bounds begin\n    @test sum(MyPkg.window([1, 2, 3], 2)) == 5\nend\n\nItems of this profile reuse its workers. Another profile can use the same pool when its workers start the same way. To run an item alone in a new process, add sandbox = true:\n\n@testitem \"indexes stay in bounds, alone\" profile=:bounds sandbox=true begin\n    @test sum(MyPkg.window([1, 2, 3], 2)) == 5\nend\n\nThe configuration reference lists each key of a profile. A profile has no environment key. A configuration file that gives one is an error, and the message tells you to give the items profile = :name instead.","title":"Declare a profile"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/profiles/#The-default-profile","page":"Profiles and environments","text":"[profiles.default] configures the workers of every item without a profile, sandboxed or not. Without this table, the default profile has no flags, no environment variables and no expressions. A profile without threads uses the threads of the run.","title":"The default profile"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/profiles/#init-and-test_end","page":"Profiles and environments","text":"init runs once in each worker of the profile, before the first item. test_end runs after each attempt at an item that was not skipped, a failed attempt before its retry too. Each item runs the test_end of its own profile. Each has its own timeout, init_timeout and test_end_timeout, and this time does not count toward the timeout of the item. A failure in test_end counts toward the result of the item.","title":"init and test_end"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/profiles/#Preferences","page":"Profiles and environments","text":"preferences names a TOML file, relative to the configuration file. The file must exist. Its contents go over the LocalPreferences.toml of a temporary copy of the environment of the item. Runtests precompiles separately for the preferences of each profile.","title":"Preferences"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/profiles/#environments","page":"Profiles and environments","text":"A directory under test/ with a Project.toml or JuliaProject.toml of its own is an environment directory. The test files in it run in that environment, not in the test environment. With this, a suite can keep tests whose dependencies the test environment must not carry. Examples are Aqua or JET tests, and a group that needs other versions of packages:\n\ntest/\n  qa/\n    Project.toml                   # Aqua, JET, ...\n    aqua_tests.jl                  # its items run in the qa environment\n\nEvery test item under the directory runs in that environment, under the profile that it asks for. When environment directories are nested, the innermost directory that holds the file decides. An item in another place cannot use the environment.\n\nAn environment for tests declares no package. If its Project.toml has a name, the run warns and names the directory. Move a package that the tests take as input to test/testfixtures/. Move a test setup to test/testsetups/.\n\nThe workers run in a copy of the environment, in the temporary files of the system. The copy has these changes:\n\nThe package under test is added by its path. This also brings the packages that its [sources] names, for example its siblings in a monorepo.\nThe LocalPreferences.toml of the directory is used, with the preferences of the profile over it.\n\nRuntests writes nothing into the directory. The copy is stacked over the test environment, as Pkg.test stacks its sandbox over the active project. Thus an item loads the packages of the environment first, and can still load the packages of the test environment.\n\nRuntests resolves the copy once in each session, and again when a file that it was built from changes. A copy that does not resolve stops the run before any item runs.","title":"Run items in an environment of their own"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/profiles/#Directories-that-are-not-environments","page":"Profiles and environments","text":"These directories are never environment directories, even with a project of their own:\n\ntest/ itself, which holds the test environment,\nthe directories in test/testsetups/, which are setup packages,\nthe directories in test/testtemplates/ and test/testfixtures/,\nhidden directories, whose names start with ..\n\nDiscovery does not follow directory symlinks. A replay finds the environment of each item where its file is, in the checkout that the replay runs in.","title":"Directories that are not environments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/profiles/#Pools-of-workers","page":"Profiles and environments","text":"A run keeps a pool of workers for each start configuration that its items use. The start configuration of a worker is what it gets when it starts: Julia flags, threads, environment variables, init, preferences and environment. Thus an item with profile = :bounds in test/qa/ runs on a worker with the flags of bounds and the packages of test/qa. Two profiles that differ only in test_end share a pool, and each item still runs its own profile's test_end. Across packages of a workspace, an init must also run in the same directory for workers to be shared. Share a profile explains how the workspace root supplies one init to its packages.\n\nThe UP line of a worker names its profile when an item of the run uses a profile other than default. It names the environment of the worker when an item uses an environment directory. Output gives the details.\n\nItems of different pools run on different workers, and can run at the same time. [order] does not make them run one after the other. A chain runs its items in sequence on one worker. Thus all items of a chain must use the same profile and be in the same environment.\n\nWhen a pool has no items left, its workers can restart for another pool. A worker restarts only for a pool that the restart makes finish sooner. Of those pools, it restarts for the pool that finishes last. How a run works explains this.","title":"Pools of workers"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#failures","page":"Investigate failures","text":"This page tells you how to find out why an item failed. You can read its report and its log, and run it again with the same seed. You can also keep files that explain the failure, and step into the item with a debugger.","title":"Investigate failures"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#Read-the-report-of-the-item","page":"Investigate failures","text":"An item that did not pass gets a block with its results right after its DONE line:\n\n🔴 w1 · 12:55:14 · DONE · 1/1 · \"fails\" · FAIL ·  0.0s (92% compile) · maxrss 0.3 GiB\n┌ [1/1] FAIL \"fails\"\n│ Test Failed at test/faults_test.jl:6\n│   Expression: 1 == 2\n│ [ No captured logs\n└ @ test/faults_test.jl:5 on worker 1\n\nThe block holds the failed tests, the captured output of the item, and the path of the files that the item kept. Under logs = :issues, the default, only items that did not pass print their output. Under logs = :quiet, no item prints its output. Then read the output in the log of the item. Under logs = :eager, the output streams as the item runs and is not repeated in the block.","title":"Read the report of the item"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#Read-the-logs","page":"Investigate failures","text":"Except under logs = :eager, each run keeps what each item printed in the directory of the run, <run>.rundata, next to its run state. The closing block names the directory on its run state line. The directory also holds the failure report of each attempt that did not pass. The files reference shows the names of the files.\n\nWith logs = :eager, use the terminal or CI job log to read the item's output; Runtests does not retain an item log of that output. Failure reports and artifact evidence are still kept. Producers' output is captured in their .artifacts.log even in this mode. The configuration reference lists all logging modes.","title":"Read the logs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#Run-the-failures-again","page":"Investigate failures","text":"Runtests.runtestsf() runs the items that are failing, each starting from the seed that it failed with. This helps reproduce random inputs when the code and its sequence of random draws are unchanged. It does not restore external state, task scheduling, or a random generator that the test seeds separately, so it does not guarantee the failure will recur. Run tests explains selection, and Run states and history explains which failures remain on record.","title":"Run the failures again"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#Replay-a-recorded-run","page":"Investigate failures","text":"A run state records a run: its items, configuration, profiles, [order] and seeds. To run the same items again, give the directory of the run as replay. The run.runstate file in it also works.\n\nRuntests.read_run_state(\"path/to/the.rundata\")     # read the record\nRuntests.runtests(replay = \"path/to/the.rundata\")  # run its items again\nRuntests.runtestsf(replay = \"path/to/the.rundata\") # run its failures again\n\nA replay does these things:\n\nIt uses the items, the configuration, the profiles, the [order] and the seeds of the record. A keyword that you give replaces the recorded value.\nIt gives the workers the Julia flags of the recorded run, when these differ from the flags of this process.\nIt names each package whose version differs from the recorded environment. It does not install the recorded versions.\nIt names each recorded item that the suite no longer has. A renamed or deleted item cannot run again.\n\nruntestsf(replay = ...) selects the failures from the verdicts of that run and of the runs that started after it. Earlier runs give only durations.\n\nFor a combined workspace run, call Runtests.runtests_mono(replay = \"path/to/a.runstate\") with the run state of any participating package. The monorepo guide explains how it finds the other packages' records.\n\nA run state from CI works the same way. Download the artifact of the job, and give the path of the run state as replay. Run on CI tells you how to keep run states on CI.","title":"Replay a recorded run"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#evidence","page":"Investigate failures","text":"An item can keep files that explain a failure. There are two kinds of files: outputs that the item writes while it runs, and files that producers write after the attempt fails. A producer is a function that the item registers with Runtests.on_failure.\n\nThis example needs no packages beyond Runtests and Julia's standard library. Put it in test/evidence_test.jl and run Runtests.runtests(\"test/evidence_test.jl\"). It fails deliberately and prints the path of its evidence:\n\n@testitem \"keep a failed comparison\" begin\n    import Runtests\n    expected = [1, 2, 3]\n    actual = [1, 2, 4]\n\n    write(joinpath(Runtests.artifacts_dir(), \"input.txt\"), \"1,2,3\\n\")\n    Runtests.on_failure() do dir\n        open(joinpath(dir, \"comparison.txt\"), \"w\") do io\n            println(io, \"expected = \", expected)\n            println(io, \"actual = \", actual)\n        end\n    end\n\n    @test actual == expected\nend\n\nThe attempt keeps files/input.txt and producer_1/comparison.txt. Change actual to [1, 2, 3] and run again: the producer does not run, and the passing attempt's files are deleted. The earlier failure's evidence remains with its own run state.\n\nThe same pattern applies to images and compiled programs. These illustrations use application-specific functions such as render_scene, save and compile_program:\n\n@testitem \"image matches reference\" begin\n    import Runtests\n    actual = render_scene()\n    reference = load_reference()\n\n    Runtests.on_failure() do dir\n        save(joinpath(dir, \"actual.png\"), actual)\n        save(joinpath(dir, \"difference.png\"), difference(actual, reference))\n    end\n\n    @test actual ≈ reference\nend\n\n@testitem \"compiled program runs\" begin\n    import Runtests\n    dir = Runtests.artifacts_dir()\n    executable = compile_program(dir)\n    @test success(run(ignorestatus(`$executable`)))\nend","title":"Keep evidence from a failed attempt"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#artifacts_dir","page":"Investigate failures","text":"Runtests.artifacts_dir() makes the files/ directory of the attempt and returns its path. It returns the same directory each time within one attempt. Write outputs there directly. Runtests copies nothing.","title":"artifacts_dir"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#on_failure","page":"Investigate failures","text":"Runtests.on_failure(f) registers a producer and returns nothing. It does not call f. If the attempt fails, Runtests calls each producer once, in the order of registration. Each producer gets a directory of its own, producer_<k>/. The test chooses the names of the files in both directories.","title":"on_failure"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#When-an-attempt-fails","page":"Investigate failures","text":"An attempt fails when a test fails or the item throws, in its body or in the test_end of its profile. Producers can run only if the worker survives to return the result. A skipped item, a test marked as broken and an attempt that passes do not run producers. An attempt that passes deletes what it wrote.\n\nProducers run after the body and its profile's test_end finish and the run records the verdict. They see their values as they are when they run, not as they were at registration. A file that a mktempdir() do block deletes is gone by then. Write such a file to artifacts_dir() instead.\n\nCollection has its own time budget and does not count toward the item's measured time or timeout. It does take time in the run and occupies the worker until it ends. The on_item callback receives :finished before collection begins; that event does not mean the evidence is ready.\n\nA producer that throws, or a @test in a producer that fails, goes into the .artifacts.log file. It does not change the verdict, and the next producer still runs. All producers of an attempt together have artifacts_timeout seconds; this run setting defaults to timeout. If they take longer, or the worker stops, the run replaces the worker and keeps the files written so far. If the body or test_end times out or loses its worker, producers do not run. When producers were registered, the .artifacts.log file says why.\n\nA worker lost during collection also loses a chain's process state. The remaining members cannot continue on that process; the usual chain retry rules apply.\n\nwarning: Warning\nA producer running in the caller's process, under workers = 0 or while debugging, has no enforceable timeout. Sandboxed items that still get a worker under workers = 0 keep the collection timeout.","title":"When an attempt fails"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#Where-the-evidence-goes","page":"Investigate failures","text":"Each attempt keeps its evidence next to its log, in the directory of the run. A retry gets a directory of its own. Thus a failed attempt keeps its evidence when a later attempt passes. The report of the failure and the closing block of the run print the path. The files reference shows the layout.\n\nThe evidence is deleted with the run state. RUNTESTS_ARTIFACT_DIR copies it with the logs; Run on CI explains how to upload it, including evidence from failed attempts whose retries passed. A pasted item keeps its evidence in a new directory in the temporary files of the system. So does an item that you step into with Runtests.debug. The REPL prints its path. Delete the directory when you no longer need it.","title":"Where the evidence goes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#Limits","page":"Investigate failures","text":"Both functions work only in the body of an item, and in the tasks that the body starts and waits for. They throw an ArgumentError in init, in skip, in test_end, in a producer and outside an item. They also throw in a task that the item did not wait for, if the task calls them after the body returned. The items of a chain do not share evidence, because the directory of a passing item is deleted.\n\nA registered producer keeps what it refers to, and the globals of the item, until collection ends or the producers are discarded after a pass. A test_end such as GC.gc(true) does not release that memory. A passed attempt releases it before its worker runs the next item. The producer's serialization or other expensive work happens only after a failure; registration still retains the references and writes a small note that producers are waiting.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/failures/#Step-into-an-item-with-a-debugger","page":"Investigate failures","text":"With Debugger.jl loaded, Runtests.debug steps into one test item in the current process. Without a name, it steps into the most recent failure, with the seed that it failed with. Work at the REPL tells you how.","title":"Step into an item with a debugger"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/setups/#setups","page":"Share code with test setups","text":"A test setup is a module in test/testsetups/ that test items load with using or import. Put code there that several items share, for example helper functions, constants and fixture paths.","title":"Share code with test setups"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/setups/#Write-a-setup","page":"Share code with test setups","text":"Put the module in test/testsetups/Name.jl, or in test/testsetups/Name/src/Name.jl:\n\n# test/testsetups/MySetups.jl\nmodule MySetups\nexport ping\nping() = true\nend\n\nAn item loads it as a package:\n\n@testitem \"server answers\" begin\n    using MySetups\n    @test ping()\nend\n\nRuntests finds the using and import statements of the items. Before it starts the workers, it precompiles the setups that the selected items need.","title":"Write a setup"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/setups/#Top-level-code-runs-at-precompilation","page":"Share code with test setups","text":"A setup is precompiled like any package. Its top-level code runs when Julia builds or rebuilds its precompile cache. Workers normally load that cache. Put work that must happen in each process in the __init__ function of the module.\n\nAll items that a worker runs share the globals of the setup. Thus a mutable fixture in a setup carries changes from one item to the next. Build mutable fixtures inside the items.","title":"Top-level code runs at precompilation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/setups/#Convert-setups-into-packages","page":"Share code with test setups","text":"Runtests.setups_to_packages() makes each setup a package of its own. Runtests.chores() does this conversion as one of its steps. The conversion does these changes:\n\nIt moves Name.jl to Name/src/Name.jl.\nIt writes Name/Project.toml with a UUID and with the packages that the setup imports, under [deps].\nIt replaces @__DIR__ with pkgdir(MyPkg, \"test\", \"testsetups\"), where MyPkg is the package under test. Thus a fixture path works also when you paste the code into the REPL.\nWhen a path rewrite needs the package under test, it adds import MyPkg if the setup does not already bind that module.\n\nWithout a project and a UUID, all setups with the same name share one precompile cache in each depot. Each checkout and each set of Julia flags then writes over the cache of the others. After the conversion, each checkout has its own cache, and a run precompiles the setups for the Julia flags of each profile.\n\nA package can import only what its [deps] lists. Thus you must convert again after a setup starts to import a new package. The conversion adds what is missing, changes nothing else and keeps the UUIDs. The conversion cannot see an import in code that the setup generates or evaluates at load time. Add such an import to [deps] yourself.\n\nThe conversion writes nothing unless it can convert every setup. These problems stop it, and it reports all of them at once:\n\nan import that neither the test environment nor the standard library provides,\nan include with a relative string literal, such as include(\"helpers.jl\"),\na use of @__FILE__.","title":"Convert setups into packages"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/setups/#setup-paths","page":"Share code with test setups","text":"In a flat setup file, test/testsetups/Name.jl, @__DIR__ is test/testsetups/. Converting that file rewrites the expression to pkgdir(MyPkg, \"test\", \"testsetups\"), preserving its original meaning. Thus joinpath(@__DIR__, \"..\", \"data\") becomes pkgdir(MyPkg, \"test\", \"data\").\n\nIn a setup that you write directly as test/testsetups/Name/src/Name.jl, @__DIR__ names that src/ directory; conversion does not relocate or rewrite this file. For fixture paths that should be independent of the setup's layout, import the package under test and use pkgdir(MyPkg, \"test\", \"testfixtures\", \"filename\").\n\nIn an item, @__DIR__ and include are relative to the test file.","title":"Paths in a setup"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/setups/#Lists-that-templates-share","page":"Share code with test setups","text":"A test template can use a list from a setup, by a qualified name such as MySetups.PERIODS. The template must import the setup in its body. Put a list that several templates share in a setup.","title":"Lists that templates share"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/editors/#editors","page":"Use an editor","text":"Runtests has an extension for VS Code, and a server that any editor can drive. This page tells you how to install the extension and what it does.","title":"Use an editor"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/editors/#The-VS-Code-extension","page":"Use an editor","text":"The extension shows test items in the Test Explorer of VS Code and runs them there. For one package, it shows each file under test/ with its items. For a workspace of packages, it groups files and items by package. You can run items from the tree or from the gutter next to the code. Results show while the run goes on, and a failure is marked at the line of the @test that failed. Cancel stops the run and its workers at once.\n\nThe output of a run goes to the Test Results panel. It is the output that Runtests writes in a terminal, with colors. The logs value in runtests.run.options controls which item output the panel shows, as it does for runtests.\n\nThe Run Failed Tests button in the toolbar of the Test Explorer runs what Runtests.runtestsf() runs. These are the items whose last run did not pass them, from the editor, the terminal or CI. The Rerun Failed Tests command of VS Code knows only the runs of this window.","title":"The VS Code extension"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/editors/#Install-the-extension","page":"Use an editor","text":"The extension is plain JavaScript with no dependencies, so there is nothing to build. The extension is in editors/vscode of the Runtests repository. Use one of these two ways:\n\nTo try it from a checkout, open editors/vscode in VS Code and press F5. In the window that opens, open a package that uses Runtests.\nTo install it for yourself, link the directory into the extensions of VS Code, and reload the window:\nln -s \"$PWD/editors/vscode\" ~/.vscode/extensions/runtests.runtests-0.1.0\n\nThe server's environment must be able to run using Runtests. For one package, the extension uses its test/Project.toml when there is one, and the package's environment otherwise. If Runtests is only in [extras] and [targets], set runtests.environment to a project that declares it; the server will still build the package's test environment for the tests.\n\nFor a workspace folder, the extension tries the root test project, the root project, then each member's test project, choosing the first whose [deps] declares Runtests. Set runtests.environment if none of them does. By default, a folder is treated as a combined workspace when its root project lists members besides its own test; runtests.monorepo overrides this detection.","title":"Install the extension"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/editors/#VS-Code-settings-of-the-extension","page":"Use an editor","text":"VS Code setting Meaning\nruntests.julia.executable The julia that runs the server. By default, the julia.executablePath of the Julia extension, or julia on the PATH.\nruntests.julia.args Arguments that go to julia first, for example [\"+1.12\"] for a juliaup channel.\nruntests.environment The environment that the server runs in, as an absolute path or relative to the folder. It must contain Runtests.\nruntests.monorepo auto (default), on, or off. In auto, serve a folder as a combined workspace when its root project lists members besides its own test.\nruntests.run.options The editor protocol's run options sent with every run, for example {\"workers\": 2}.\nruntests.config A configuration file to read in place of test/TestItems.toml, relative to the folder. It applies only to a single-package server; a combined server warns and reads each package's own file. Restart the server after changing this setting.\n\nOther run settings come from the configuration file and environment variables, as for Runtests.runtests(). For example, set artifacts_timeout in [run] or RUNTESTS_ARTIFACTS_TIMEOUT; the editor's options field does not accept it.\n\nThe extension has three commands: Runtests: Run Failed Tests, Runtests: Restart Server and Runtests: Show Log. Edits to the root project refresh the tree; a change of server mode or runtests.environment restarts the server. Restart it manually after changing the Julia executable, Julia arguments or runtests.config, or after updating Runtests in the server's environment. The extension requires editor protocol version 3 and stops with an error if that Julia environment loads a server of another protocol version.","title":"VS Code settings of the extension"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/editors/#What-the-extension-does-not-do","page":"Use an editor","text":"The extension has no debugger and no coverage view. The Julia extension also finds @testitem declarations, and shows them in its own tree, run by its own runner. Items with the keywords of Runtests (timeout, retries, chain, sandbox, failfast) are errors there.","title":"What the extension does not do"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/editors/#Drive-Runtests-from-another-editor","page":"Use an editor","text":"Runtests.serve(path) lets an editor drive the suite of one package; Runtests.serve_mono(root) serves a workspace of packages. The server lists test items with their locations and keywords. It runs the items that the editor asks for, a group, or a replay of a run state. It reports the start and the outcome of each item while the run goes on, and cancels a run when the editor asks.\n\nThe server uses one JSON object for each line. Commands come on stdin, and events go to stdout. Everything written for people goes to stderr.\n\nFrom a package whose test/Project.toml declares Runtests:\n\ncd MyPackage && julia --project=test -e 'using Runtests; Runtests.serve()'\n\nFrom a workspace root whose lib/A/test/Project.toml declares Runtests:\n\njulia --project=lib/A/test -e 'using Runtests; Runtests.serve_mono(pwd())'\n\nThe editor protocol describes each command and event. Check the version in its first hello event before sending commands; version 3 identifies items by package and name.","title":"Drive Runtests from another editor"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/writing/#writing","page":"Write test items","text":"This page tells you where test files go, how to write a test item, and what its keywords do. A test item is a named block of tests that runs alone, in a module of its own, on a worker process.","title":"Write test items"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/writing/#Lay-out-the-test-directory","page":"Write test items","text":"Runtests reads the files under the test/ directory of the package. Each Julia file there must belong to one of these categories:\n\ntest/\n  runtests.jl                      # the entry point that Pkg.test runs\n  solver_test.jl                   # a test file: @testitem declarations\n  sub/parser_tests.jl              # a test file in a subdirectory\n  periods_tests.jl                 # an expansion that Runtests.chores() wrote\n  testsetups/\n    MySetups.jl                    # a test setup, loaded with `using MySetups`\n  testtemplates/\n    periods_tests_template.jl      # a test template: @testtemplate declarations\n  qa/\n    Project.toml                   # the environment of the test files in qa/\n    aqua_tests.jl\n  testfixtures/                    # input for the tests, never read as tests\n    Example/Project.toml\n    reference.png\n  TestItems.toml                   # the configuration of the suite (optional)\n\nThe name of a test file ends in _test.jl or _tests.jl. A Julia file under test/ that is in no category stops the run with an error. Thus a misnamed test file cannot go untested without a message. Runtests ignores hidden files and hidden directories.\n\nRuntests finds items by parsing the test files. It does not evaluate them in the process that calls runtests. A test file holds @testitem declarations and nothing else. Put imports and other code inside an item, or put shared code in a test setup.","title":"Lay out the test directory"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/writing/#Test-fixtures","page":"Write test items","text":"test/testfixtures/ holds input for the tests. Examples are packages, projects, source files that are broken on purpose, reference images and data. A run, a dry run, chores and an editor never read this directory, never count its tests and never add it to LOAD_PATH. Only the directory with this name at the top of test/ has this meaning.\n\nA package in test/testfixtures/ runs as a suite of its own when you give a path inside it alone, for example Runtests.runtests(\"test/testfixtures/Example\"). A path into test/testfixtures/ together with paths of the suite is an error.","title":"Test fixtures"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/writing/#Directories-with-a-project-of-their-own","page":"Write test items","text":"Each other directory under test/ that has its own Project.toml or JuliaProject.toml is an environment directory. The test files in it run in that environment, not in the test environment. Profiles and environments tells you more. test/Project.toml is the test environment, and the packages in test/testsetups/ are setups, not environment directories. A project that is input for the tests goes in test/testfixtures/. If the project of an environment directory declares a package, the run warns. A package there is usually a fixture or a setup in the wrong place.","title":"Directories with a project of their own"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/writing/#Write-a-test-item","page":"Write test items","text":"@testitem \"adds numbers\" tags=[:fast] begin\n    @test 1 + 1 == 2\nend\n\n@testitem \"against a database\" chain=:db timeout=600 retries=1 begin\n    using MySetups          # test/testsetups/MySetups.jl\n    @test MySetups.ping()\nend\n\nThe name of an item is a string literal that is not blank. It must be unique across the suite. Test and the package under test are in the scope of the item, so @test and @testset work. The test environment does not have to declare Test.","title":"Write a test item"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/writing/#Keywords","page":"Write test items","text":"Keywords come between the name and begin. The syntax reference lists them all. This list summarizes what they do:\n\ntags = [:a, :b] gives the item tags that a run can select by.\ntimeout = N gives each attempt N seconds. It replaces the run's timeout for this item.\nretries = N gives the item N more attempts after a failure, from 0 to 126.\nskip = expr skips the item when expr gives true.\nfailfast = true stops the item at its first failed test.\nchain = :name runs the items of one chain in sequence, on one worker.\nsandbox = true runs the item alone in a new process, which stops after the item.\nprofile = :name runs the item on workers with the configuration of the profile name.\n\nEach keyword except skip must be a literal. Arithmetic on numeric literals is also a literal, for example timeout = 5*60. An incorrect value is an error. For example, timeout = true is not one second.\n\nskip can be any expression. The worker evaluates it before the body, with Test and the package in scope, but before the imports of the body. It must give a Bool. An exception or a value of another type is an error of the item.","title":"Keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/writing/#Retries,-chains-and-sandboxes","page":"Write test items","text":"A retry runs on the same worker, except in three cases: the worker timed out, the worker crashed, or the item has sandbox = true. A chain retries from its first item. All items in a chain must use the same profile and be in the same environment. sandbox = true and chain do not go together.\n\nThe items of a profile share the workers of that profile, and of every profile whose workers start the same way. If an item needs a new process of its own, add sandbox = true. The two keywords combine: profile = :bounds sandbox = true runs the item alone, in a new process of the bounds profile. Neither keyword chooses the packages of the item. The location of its file does.","title":"Retries, chains and sandboxes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/writing/#How-the-body-runs","page":"Write test items","text":"The body of an item runs at the top level of a new module, with the soft scope of the REPL. Thus x = 1 followed by a loop that changes x works as it does at the prompt. As in a script, the variables of the body are untyped globals.\n\nwarning: Warning\nPut code whose speed or allocations a test measures inside a function or a let block. In the body, the measurement includes the access to the untyped globals.\n\nJulia measures a plain call such as @allocated f(x, y) from inside f, when the function and the arguments are names or literals. Julia measures any other expression where it stands, with the global access. These examples include a qualified name, a keyword argument and a begin block. Use a let block for them:\n\n@testitem \"summing allocates nothing\" begin\n    let\n        x = rand(10)\n        @test @allocated(sum(x; init = 0.0)) == 0\n    end\nend\n\nWhen the item ends, the run releases what its nonconstant globals refer to. An item that registers failure-artifact producers retains the values they need through test_end and artifact collection. A const keeps its value for as long as the worker runs.","title":"How the body runs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/writing/#Items-on-one-worker","page":"Write test items","text":"A worker runs one item at a time, and runs many items one after the other. Later items on that worker can see changes to ENV, the global logger and loaded packages. A working-directory change can persist when an item runs directly in the worker's directory. In a combined run, an item of a member package below the workspace root temporarily enters that package's root and restores the worker's directory afterward; a root-package item can leave it changed. Restore process state that an item changes, with withenv or try and finally. If an item cannot restore it, give the item sandbox = true.\n\nItems on different workers run at the same time. If two items can write to the same path, make each of them use its own mktempdir().\n\nEach item draws its random numbers from the seed of the run, combined with the name of the item. The run prints its seed. If the expected values of an item come from a fixed seed, call Random.seed! in the item.","title":"Items on one worker"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/writing/#Use-Runtests-inside-an-item","page":"Write test items","text":"Test infrastructure can ask Runtests about the item that runs. Examples are code that makes temporary directories, finds fixture paths or switches off telemetry:\n\nimport Runtests               # in the body of the item\nRuntests.current_testitem()   # a TestItemInfo, or nothing\n\ncurrent_testitem() gives the name, file, line, attempt and profile of the item. The tasks that the item starts see the item too. A task that started before the item does not, for example a service that a profile's init started. A subprocess does not see it either.\n\nwarning: Warning\nDo not make library code change what it does because it is under test. Then the tests no longer test the library.\n\nTo keep files that explain a failure, use Runtests.on_failure and Runtests.artifacts_dir. Investigate failures tells you how.","title":"Use Runtests inside an item"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/monorepos/#monorepos","page":"Run the suites of a monorepo","text":"A monorepo is a repository that holds several Julia packages, for example a root package and packages under lib/. Runtests.runtests_mono runs the test suites of these packages in one run: one plan, one set of workers and one report. Items of different packages share workers when their workers start the same way. A combined run needs Julia 1.12 or later.","title":"Run the suites of a monorepo"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/monorepos/#Set-up-the-workspace","page":"Run the suites of a monorepo","text":"A combined run runs the packages of a Julia workspace. All members of a workspace share one manifest. The root Project.toml lists each package and the test project of each package under [workspace]:\n\n[workspace]\nprojects = [\"lib/A\", \"lib/A/test\", \"lib/B\", \"lib/B/test\"]\n\nA package can also list its own test under a [workspace] of its own. The root then lists only the package.\n\nEach package that takes part must have a test/Project.toml (or test/JuliaProject.toml), and that project must be a member of the workspace. Tests that use only [extras] and [targets] cannot take part. The workspace manifest must hold Runtests: add Runtests to [deps] of a test project or of the root project, and resolve the workspace.","title":"Set up the workspace"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/monorepos/#Run-the-tests","page":"Run the suites of a monorepo","text":"At the REPL, activate a project of the workspace that has Runtests in its [deps], then call Runtests.runtests_mono(). It finds the workspace root from the active project. For example, from the workspace root when lib/A/test/Project.toml declares Runtests:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"lib/A/test\")\nPkg.instantiate()\nusing Runtests\nRuntests.runtests_mono()\n\nTo run the combined suites with Pkg.test of a root package, that package's own test environment must also contain Runtests. Put this in its test/runtests.jl:\n\nusing Runtests\nRuntests.runtests_mono()\n\nRuntests instantiates the workspace once. Then it writes one environment for the shared workers. This environment holds the test dependencies of all packages, in the versions of the workspace manifest, and Julia loads it without a resolve.\n\nruntests_mono takes the selections of runtests. name, tags and group select within each package. A group or a tag that only some packages have is no error. A path selects within the package that holds it. The keyword packages takes package names, or directories relative to the workspace root, and runs only those packages:\n\nRuntests.runtests_mono(; packages = [\"A\", \"lib/B\"], group = \"fast\")","title":"Run the tests"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/monorepos/#Choose-the-packages","page":"Run the suites of a monorepo","text":"By default, every package of the workspace that has a suite takes part. A table [monorepo] in test/TestItems.toml of the workspace root changes this:\n\n[monorepo]\npackages = [\"lib/*\", \".\"]\nisolated = [\"lib/Legacy\"]\n\npackages lists the packages that take part, as directories or globs relative to the root. isolated lists packages that run in their own test environment, on workers of their own. If two workspace members have the same package name, use packages to leave one out; a combined run cannot identify both by name. The header of the run lists the packages, names the isolated ones, and says why each other package of the workspace does not take part.","title":"Choose the packages"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/monorepos/#What-the-packages-share","page":"Run the suites of a monorepo","text":"Two profiles share workers when their workers start the same way: the same flags, threads, environment variables, preferences and environment. This is true for profiles of one package and for profiles of two packages. A profile with an init keeps workers of its own package, because init runs in the root of its package. A shared profile is the exception. An environment directory and an isolated package also keep workers of their own.\n\nEach item runs in the root of its own package and imports its own package. It runs under the profiles, setups and settings of its own package. test_end runs under the test_end_timeout of the package of the item.\n\nThe settings of the run itself come from the call, the RUNTESTS_ variables and [run] of the workspace root, in that order. These settings are workers, memory_threshold, logs, verbose, monitor, monitor_interval, full_stacktraces, full_names, testset_name, coverage, seed and failfast. Such a setting in the [run] of another package has no effect, and the header of the run says so. The other settings belong to each package. A keyword or a variable gives every package its value. Otherwise each package keeps the value of its own file.","title":"What the packages share"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/monorepos/#shared-profiles","page":"Run the suites of a monorepo","text":"To give the packages of a workspace one profile, init included, declare it in the test/TestItems.toml of the workspace root with monorepo_default = true:\n\n[profiles.default]\nmonorepo_default = true\ninit = \"global WORKER_STARTED = time()\"\n\nA package of the workspace that does not declare a profile of this name uses the shared profile. A package that declares it with the same init and test_end uses the shared profile too. The code must be the same, but the layout and the comments can be different. A package whose init or test_end differs keeps its own profile. Profiles with different test_end expressions can still share workers when their start configuration and init directory are the same; test_end runs after each item.\n\nThe init of a shared profile runs in the workspace root, in the runs of each package and in combined runs. Thus the packages that use the profile share its workers. Shared profiles need Julia 1.12 or later, which has workspaces.\n\nRuntests.chores_mono() shows the groups of workers. The items of one group can share a worker, and the items of two groups cannot. Each group names its profiles and their packages. The report also names the copies of a shared profile that a package can delete. It also names a profile that the root declares the same way as a package, and that monorepo_default = true would share.","title":"Share a profile"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/monorepos/#What-stops-a-combined-run","page":"Run the suites of a monorepo","text":"These problems stop a combined run before a worker starts:\n\nA using or an import that a shared worker can load and the test environment of its own package cannot. Such an import names a package, a test dependency or a setup of another package. Items, setups, init and test_end are read. The error names the file, the import and the project that must declare it.\nTwo selected packages with a setup of the same name. A shared worker finds a setup by its name, so it would load only one of them.\nA setup with the name of a package in the environment of the shared workers, for example a test dependency of another package. A shared worker loads that package for the name, but the run of its own package loads the setup. Rename the setup, or put one of the two packages in isolated.\nTwo packages that declare one dependency name with two different UUIDs.\nTwo packages whose test/LocalPreferences.toml set one preference to two different values.\nTwo participating packages with the same package name. An editor and a combined run identify an item by its package name and item name.\n\nTo fix 4 or 5 for a package that cannot change, put the package in isolated.\n\nThe import check reads the code as it is written. It does not see an import that code builds at run time, for example with Core.eval, or an import in a file that include reads. Such an import can load a test dependency of another package in a combined run, and fail in the run of its own package.","title":"What stops a combined run"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/monorepos/#Run-states-and-replay","page":"Run the suites of a monorepo","text":"A combined run writes one run state for each package, in the run state directory of that package. Each run state holds only the items of its package. Thus runtestsf() in a package runs the failures of that package from the combined run, and the next run of the package plans with the durations of the combined run.\n\nThe closing block gives one line for each package: its items, those that did not pass, and the directory of its run. To replay a combined run, give the directory of the run of any of its packages:\n\nRuntests.runtests_mono(; replay = \"/path/to/the.rundata\")\n\nThe replay finds the run states of the other packages of the run. It runs their items again with their configuration and seeds. When the run state of a package is gone, the replay runs the other packages and says that it is a partial replay.","title":"Run states and replay"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/monorepos/#Maintain-the-suites-and-work-at-the-REPL","page":"Run the suites of a monorepo","text":"Runtests.chores_mono() does the chores of every package that takes part. Before the chores of any package change a file, it stops for the problems that no chore fixes: two setups of one name, a dependency name with two UUIDs, and preferences that disagree. It writes one report for all packages, with paths from the workspace root. After the chores, the report shows the groups of workers and checks the imports of every package that shares workers, as a combined run does. It also names each test/runtests.jl that does more than call Runtests.runtests(), because a combined run does not run that file. Move what that file sets to test/TestItems.toml of its package. Run settings go to [run], the group that a run selects goes to default of [groups], and variables go to env of a profile. A variable whose value the file computes goes to init of the profile.\n\nRuntests.activate_mono() makes the REPL look like a shared worker. It activates the environment of the shared workers and puts the setups of every package that shares workers on LOAD_PATH. An included test item runs as an item of the package that holds its file, with that package loaded and its root as the working directory. A test item pasted at the REPL uses the package that holds the REPL's current directory, so cd into a package first. Runtests.deactivate() restores the environment and LOAD_PATH; it does not undo cd. To work with an isolated package, use Runtests.activate with that package. Work at the REPL has an example.","title":"Maintain the suites and work at the REPL"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/monorepos/#Limits","page":"Run the suites of a monorepo","text":"With workers = 0, a combined run runs one package only, because this process holds one test environment.\n\nA shared worker keeps the packages that it loaded for earlier items, from any package. An item that needs a clean process must use sandbox = true. Put a package whose tests react to the code of other packages in isolated.\n\nWith coverage = true, the run writes one lcov.info at the workspace root. It counts the src/ and ext/ directories of every package.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/porting/#porting","page":"Port an existing suite","text":"This page tells you how to convert a suite that uses Test.jl testsets, ReTestItems or TestItemRunner to Runtests. It also tells you what changes for a test when a testset becomes a test item.","title":"Port an existing suite"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/porting/#The-port,-step-by-step","page":"Port an existing suite","text":"A port rewrites the suite as test items, and changes no test. It then shows that the ported suite runs the same tests, with the same outcomes, as the original. The Runtests repository has a guide for coding agents that does a port in this way. A person can follow it too. Its steps are:\n\nMake an environment outside the repository that has Runtests.\nRecord what the suite does now: run it as CI does, and count each test and each outcome.\nAdd Runtests to the test dependencies of the package.\nConvert the suite, with the scripts below.\nMake test/runtests.jl call Runtests.runtests().\nPut shared helpers in test/testsetups/ and source or package fixtures in test/testfixtures/. Port the tests of a nested environment in place: they run in that environment. Check each remaining Julia file against the test-directory rules; do not hide tests to make discovery pass.\nRun Runtests.chores(), then the suite.\nShow that the numbers of passed, failed, errored and broken tests did not change.\n\nBefore you run Runtests on a copy of a package, set RUNTESTS_RUNSTATE_DIR to a scratch directory. A copy has the identity of the package. Without the variable, the runs of the copy go into the run history of the package.","title":"The port, step by step"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/porting/#The-conversion-scripts","page":"Port an existing suite","text":"The guide has two scripts in skills/port-to-runtests/scripts/. Each script edits the test directory that you give it, in place. Give it a copy. The commands below run from a checkout of the Runtests repository; replace /path/to/copied-package/test with the copy's test directory.\n\nport_testsets.jl converts a Test.jl suite. Each top-level @testset \"name\" begin ... end becomes @testitem \"name\" begin ... end, adding a let block where the script detects a need to preserve local scope. It reads the suite as runtests.jl runs it, with the files that it includes. Definitions at the top of a file go into a setup module that the items of the file load.\n\njulia skills/port-to-runtests/scripts/port_testsets.jl /path/to/copied-package/test\n\nport_retestitems.jl converts a ReTestItems suite. The items stay as they are. These parts change around them:\n\nEach @testsetup module S ... end becomes testsetups/S.jl.\nsetup = [S, T] on an item becomes using S, T at the top of its body.\nskip = :(expr) becomes skip = expr, because Runtests evaluates the expression itself.\ndefault_imports and _id go away, because Runtests always imports Test and the package.\nA file of items with another name becomes <stem>_tests.jl.\n\njulia skills/port-to-runtests/scripts/port_retestitems.jl /path/to/copied-package/test\n\nEach script prints what it moved, and a list of what a person must look at.","title":"The conversion scripts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/porting/#What-changes-when-a-testset-becomes-an-item","page":"Port an existing suite","text":"A test item runs in a different way from a testset. These differences can change what a test does:\n\nTopic In a testset In a test item\nScope The body is a local scope, with typed local variables. The body runs at the top level of a module, with soft scope, and its variables are untyped globals. Put code that measures speed or allocations in a let block or a function.\nMain Every file ran in Main, in one process, in order. Each file saw what earlier files defined. Each item has its own module, on one of several workers, in any order. Import what each item uses, in the item or in a setup.\nState A variable, file or value that one testset left reached the testsets after it. Items share only setups and the process of their worker. Make each item build what it needs, or put dependent items in one chain.\nProcess Changes to ENV, the logger, the working directory or the loaded packages lasted until the suite ended. Items on a worker run one after the other, and see what earlier items changed. Restore changes, or use sandbox = true.\nPrinted types Types printed without their module, where Main imported it. Main imports nothing, so messages print types with their module, for example FixedPointDecimals.FixedDecimal{Int64, 100}.\nRandom numbers A Random.seed! in runtests.jl decided every draw. Each item draws from the seed of the run combined with its name. An item that expects values from a fixed seed calls Random.seed! itself.\nThreads The process had the threads that julia started with, usually 1. Workers start with --threads=2,1. Set threads under [run], or use a profile.\nSetup code Helper code at the top of a file ran when the file was included. Top-level setup code runs when its precompile cache is built or rebuilt. Per-process initialization belongs in __init__. All items on a worker share the globals of the setup. See test setups.\nPaths @__DIR__ named the helper's directory. Moving a helper into a setup changes its directory. Check its paths against the setup path rules. In an item, @__DIR__ and include are relative to the test file.\nParallel runs Testsets ran one at a time. Items run at the same time. A path that two items can write to becomes a mktempdir().\nCrashes An exit() or a crash ended the suite. It ends only that worker. The item counts as an error, and the run continues.","title":"What changes when a testset becomes an item"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/porting/#Rules-for-a-port","page":"Port an existing suite","text":"Follow these rules during a port:\n\nDo not change what a test does to make it pass, and do not delete a test. If a test cannot pass under Runtests, leave it as it is and report it.\nDo not change src/.\nDo not edit an expansion that chores wrote. Edit the template and run chores again.","title":"Rules for a port"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/templates/#templates","page":"Generate test items from a template","text":"A test template is a file of loops, each of which makes one test item for each iteration. Use a template when a list of values must give test items that run independently. Runtests.chores() runs the loops and writes ordinary test items into a test file. This file is the expansion of the template.","title":"Generate test items from a template"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/templates/#Write-a-template","page":"Generate test items from a template","text":"Templates go in test/testtemplates/. The name of a template ends in _test_template.jl or _tests_template.jl. A template holds @testtemplate declarations and nothing else.\n\nThis example uses Dates, so add Dates to the test dependencies first:\n\n# test/testtemplates/periods_tests_template.jl\n@testtemplate \"doubling a $P of $n\" tags=[:gen] for P in (Dates.Day, Dates.Month), n in 1:2\n    using Dates\n    @test $P($n) + $P($n) == $P(2 * $n)\nend","title":"Write a template"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/templates/#Expand-the-template","page":"Generate test items from a template","text":"Run Runtests.chores(). It writes test/periods_tests.jl, the name of the template without _template. The file holds four ordinary @testitem declarations. The first loop variable changes slowest: Day with 1 and 2, then Month with 1 and 2. The first item is:\n\n@testitem \"doubling a Day of 1\" tags=[:gen] begin\n    using Dates\n    @test Day(1) + Day(1) == Day(2 * 1)\nend\n\nCommit the expansion. An installed package needs it to run Pkg.test(). When the cases change, edit the template and run Runtests.chores() again. Do not edit the expansion yourself.","title":"Expand the template"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/templates/#What-the-loop-can-see","page":"Generate test items from a template","text":"chores runs the loops in a separate process, with the test environment and the setups available. The loop sees Test, the package under test, and the packages that the body imports, by the name of the module only. In the example, the loop writes Dates.Day. The item writes Day, because its body has using Dates.\n\nThe loop does not see the names that using Dates exports. Put a list that several templates share in a setup, and refer to it by a qualified name, such as MySetups.PERIODS. The body must import that setup.","title":"What the loop can see"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/templates/#Interpolation","page":"Generate test items from a template","text":"A $ in a template puts a value of the loop into the item. The meaning depends on where the $ is:\n\nLocation Meaning\nName: \"case $n\" The value of the iteration goes into the literal name of the item.\nBody or keywords: $n The value of the loop variable goes in as Julia code.\nKeywords: $(expression) The expansion evaluates the expression once for each iteration, in the scope of the loop.\nBody: other $ expressions The expansion leaves them for the macros of the body, for example @btime.\nQuoted expressions The expansion leaves every $ as it is.\n\nRuntests writes an inserted value with repr, as the module of the item sees it with the imports of the body. The text must evaluate back to an equal value there. A value that cannot do this, for example a closure, is an error. Runtests adds parentheses where they are necessary to keep the meaning of the code around the value.\n\nThe loop variables are not bound in the item. Write $P to insert the value, or P = $P to give the item a variable named P. A P without $ is an error, unless the body binds P itself. Inside a string in the body, write \"$($P)\" to insert the value.","title":"Interpolation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/templates/#Compute-a-keyword-during-expansion","page":"Generate test items from a template","text":"A $(...) in a keyword is computed during the expansion:\n\n# test/testtemplates/cases_tests_template.jl\n@testtemplate \"case $n\" skip=$(n == 2) for n in 1:3\n    @test $n != 2\nend\n\nThe expansion writes skip=true for case 2 and skip=false for the other cases. The code outside $(...) stays part of the keyword, and the worker evaluates it when the item runs. For example, skip=Sys.iswindows() || $(n == 2) asks the operating system when the item runs. A list of known failures works as skip=$(n in MySetups.KNOWN_BAD), if the body imports MySetups.","title":"Compute a keyword during expansion"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/templates/#Keep-expansions-current","page":"Generate test items from a template","text":"Only Runtests.chores() expands templates. A test run and Runtests.chores(dry_run = true) read the stamp of each expansion, and do not run the template. A test run refuses to start if one of these conditions is true:\n\nan expansion is missing,\na template changed after its expansion,\nan expansion was edited by hand,\nthe template of an expansion was deleted.\n\nLine endings, a byte-order mark and trailing white space at the end of the file do not change the stamp.\n\nchores evaluates every template each time, because the data of a template can change while its text stays the same. It writes only the expansions that changed. It reports an expansion that was edited by hand, for you to resolve.\n\nOn CI, run Runtests.chores() and check for changed, deleted, and newly generated files. The CI example catches untracked expansions as well as changes to committed ones.","title":"Keep expansions current"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/templates/#Templates-that-cannot-expand","page":"Generate test items from a template","text":"chores reports a template that it cannot expand, and writes nothing for that template. A person must correct it. These are examples:\n\na loop or a keyword that throws,\na name that two iterations give,\na @testitem in a template,\na loop that uses a name that only a using in the body brings in.\n\n@testtemplate evaluated anywhere else than in a template that chores expands throws an error.","title":"Templates that cannot expand"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#configuration-keys","page":"Configuration","text":"This page lists every configuration key of a run, every key of test/TestItems.toml, and the public environment variables for configuring Runtests. Configure runs explains how to use them.","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#Precedence","page":"Configuration","text":"With runtests, each value comes from the first source that sets it, in this order:\n\nthe keyword of runtests,\nthe environment variable RUNTESTS_<KEY>,\nthe [run] table of test/TestItems.toml, or of the file that config names,\nthe default.\n\nAn unknown key, an unknown keyword or an incorrect value is an error before any item runs. A mistake in the call is an ArgumentError. A mistake in a file or in an environment variable is a Runtests.ConfigError.\n\nFor runtests_mono, the call and environment variables still take precedence. Run-wide keys then come from [run] at the workspace root; each package's own [run] supplies its other keys. Run-wide keys in another package's file have no effect. Run the suites of a monorepo lists the run-wide keys.","title":"Precedence"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#run-keys","page":"Configuration","text":"Each of these keys is a keyword of runtests and runtests_mono, a key of [run], and an environment variable RUNTESTS_<KEY>.\n\nKey Default Value\nworkers \"auto\" The number of workers, or \"auto\". Auto uses the number of CPUs, the threads of each worker and an assumed 4 GiB for each worker, at most 8. 0 runs ordinary items in the calling process; sandboxed items and items of a named profile still use workers. See Run tests.\nthreads \"2,1\" The --threads value of each worker, for example \"4\", \"4,1\" or \"auto\".\ntimeout 1800 Seconds for each attempt at an item.\ninit_timeout timeout Seconds for each init expression of a profile.\ntest_end_timeout timeout Seconds for each test_end expression of a profile.\nartifacts_timeout timeout Seconds for all producers of one failed attempt together.\nretries 0 More attempts after a failure, from 0 to 126.\nfailfast false Stop the run when an item or chain still fails after its allowed retries.\nitem_failfast failfast Stop each item at its first failed test.\nlogs :issues Which item output the run prints: :issues, :batched, :eager or :quiet. See below.\nverbose false Include passing testsets in the result summary. Item output is controlled by logs.\nmemory_threshold 0.9 The fraction of machine memory in use at which new items wait. Greater than 0, at most 1.\nmonitor true Monitor the memory and print progress.\nmonitor_interval 30 Seconds between progress lines when the output is not a terminal. 0 prints at each sample, five times a second.\nfull_stacktraces false Keep the frames of Runtests in the stack traces of failures.\nfull_names false Print long names in full, in place of a unique prefix.\ntestset_name \"Runtests\" The name of the testset that the run returns.\ncoverage false Merge line coverage into lcov.info. See Measure coverage.\nseed random The seed that each item combines with its name. A run without a seed chooses one and prints it.\n\nA timeout must be positive and at most 2,147,483,647 seconds. A fraction rounds up to whole seconds. Work in the calling process with workers = 0 cannot be forcibly timed out. In a TOML file or an environment variable, write a symbol as a string, for example logs = \"issues\".","title":"Run keys"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#logs","page":"Configuration","text":"Value Effect\n:issues Print the output of each item that did not pass, after its DONE line.\n:batched Print the output of every item when it ends.\n:eager Print the output of every item while it runs, without saving it to per-item log files.\n:quiet Print no item output. The output stays in the log files of the run.\n\nAn interactive session with one worker or less uses :eager by default. All other runs use :issues by default.","title":"logs"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#Call-keywords","page":"Configuration","text":"These keywords of runtests are not configuration keys and cannot go in [run]. They have no environment-variable equivalent except group, as described below.\n\nKeyword Value\nname A String for an exact name, a Regex for a partial match, or a vector or set of exact names.\ntags A symbol, a vector of symbols that an item must carry all of, or a tag expression.\ngroup The name of a group of [groups], \"all\" or \"default\". RUNTESTS_GROUP also selects a group when the call selects nothing else; see Groups.\ndry_run true prints the plan and runs nothing.\nreplay The path of a run state to run again.\nconfig The path of a file to read in place of test/TestItems.toml, relative to the current directory. The file must exist.\non_item A function that the run calls with a Runtests.ItemEvent when each attempt starts and ends.\n\nruntests_mono also accepts packages, a list of package names or directories to include. It rejects config, because a combined run reads each package's own configuration file.","title":"Call keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#TestItems.toml","page":"Configuration","text":"test/TestItems.toml is optional. It has these tables, and no others. A file that config names has the same form. Only a combined run reads [monorepo], from the file of the workspace root.","title":"TestItems.toml"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#[run]","page":"Configuration","text":"The [run] table takes the run keys above. Write each key as the keyword, for example timeout = 600. Write a symbol as a string, for example logs = \"quiet\".","title":"[run]"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#[order]","page":"Configuration","text":"Key Value\nfirst A vector of exact item names. These items start first.\nlast A vector of exact item names. These items start last.\n\nOn a run of the whole suite, each name must be an item of the suite. A run of a part of the suite accepts names of items outside the selection.","title":"[order]"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#[groups]","page":"Configuration","text":"Each key is the name of a group, and each value is a tag expression. all is reserved and cannot be declared. default is what a run selects when nothing else selects. A name that differs from all or default only in case is an error.","title":"[groups]"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#[monorepo]","page":"Configuration","text":"The workspace root's file can have this table. It selects the packages of a combined run.\n\nKey Default Value\npackages every package of the workspace with a suite A vector of directories or globs, relative to the workspace root, for example [\"lib/*\", \".\"]. The packages that take part.\nisolated [] A vector of directories or globs. These packages run in their own test environment, on workers of their own.\n\nAn entry that matches no package, or names a directory outside the workspace, is an error.","title":"[monorepo]"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#profile-keys","page":"Configuration","text":"Each profile is a table [profiles.<name>]. An item uses it with profile = :<name>. [profiles.default] configures the workers of every item without a profile, sandboxed or not.\n\nKey Default Value\njulia_args [] A vector of command-line flags for julia, for example [\"--check-bounds=yes\"].\nthreads the threads of the run The --threads value of the workers of the profile.\nenv {} A table of environment variables for the workers, for example { JULIA_DEBUG = \"Main\" }.\ninit none Julia code, as a string, that runs once in each worker before its first item. It runs in the root of the package, or in the workspace root for a shared profile.\ntest_end none Julia code, as a string, that runs after each item. A failure counts toward the item.\npreferences none A TOML file, relative to the configuration file, whose contents go over the LocalPreferences.toml of the environment. The file must exist.\nmonorepo_default false Only in the test/TestItems.toml of a workspace root. true shares the profile with the packages of the workspace, in their own runs and in combined runs. See Share a profile.","title":"Profiles"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/configuration/#environment-variables","page":"Configuration","text":"These environment variables configure Runtests:\n\nVariable Meaning\nRUNTESTS_<KEY> A run key, for example RUNTESTS_WORKERS=4, RUNTESTS_RETRIES=1 or RUNTESTS_SEED=0x1234. A true-or-false key takes true, false, 1, 0, yes or no. A keyword replaces it, and it replaces [run].\nRUNTESTS_GROUP The group to run when a call selects nothing else. An empty value counts as unset.\nRUNTESTS_RUNSTATE_DIR The directory of the run states, which holds a directory <run>.rundata for each run. The default is runtests/runs/ in the Julia depot, with a directory for each project.\nRUNTESTS_HOST The name of this machine in the run states. The default is the macOS machine name, or the hostname on other systems.\nRUNTESTS_ARTIFACT_DIR A directory into which a run copies its <run>.rundata directory, with its run state and logs, when it ends. A combined run puts each package's directory under a subdirectory named for the package.\n\nRuntests sets these variables in each worker. Do not set them yourself:\n\nVariable Meaning\nRUNTESTS_RUN_ID The ID of the run, set for each worker. Subprocesses of a worker inherit it.\nRUNTESTS_WORKER The number of the worker, as in w1.","title":"Environment variables"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/history/#history","page":"Run states and history","text":"This page explains how Runtests remembers runs. A standalone run writes one run state, a binary record of that run. A combined run writes one for each package that took part, so each package keeps its own history. Runtests uses a suite's history to plan its next run and to find its failing items.","title":"Run states and history"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/history/#What-a-run-state-records","page":"Run states and history","text":"A run writes its run states while it goes on, and prints their directory paths at the end. Even an interrupted run leaves a record of the items it completed. Each run state records these facts for its package:\n\nThe outcome, the duration and the compilation time of each item, and every attempt.\nEach start and exit of a worker, with whether the worker timed out, crashed, or got a signal from outside.\nThe commit, the Julia version and build, and the machine.\nThe configuration, the selection and the group that made it, the profiles, the preferences and the random seed.\nThe seed of each item, and a hash of its source. The hash tells an edited item from an item that only moved.\nThe Julia flags that change compiled code, such as --check-bounds and -O.\nThe environment variables JULIA_*, RUNTESTS_* and those of CI jobs, without names that look like credentials.\nThe Project.toml and the Manifest.toml of the test environment.\n\nEach package record lives as run.runstate in its own <run>.rundata directory. That directory also holds its logs: captured item output, failure reports, artifact evidence, and the reason why a run ended before its first item. Under logs = :eager, ordinary item output is streamed and is not kept there. Artifact producers still have their own captured output. The files reference shows the layout.","title":"What a run state records"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/history/#How-Runtests-uses-the-history","page":"Run states and history","text":"Only the run states in the run state directory count for the plan and for the failures. A run state in another place counts only when you give it as replay, and only for that call. Runtests orders the runs by their recorded start time, not by the file name or by when you downloaded a file.\n\nFor each item, the latest completed verdict decides whether the item is failing. The latest recorded duration of the item goes into the plan. A run that is still in progress gives durations at once. Its verdicts count for the failures and for the order only when it finishes, or when Runtests declares it dead.\n\nFailed tests, errors, timeouts, a chain cut short by a lost worker, and an item left running when its coordinator died count as failures. A skip, an explicit cancellation, or a run that never reached the item leaves the previous verdict unchanged. A later passing retry replaces the failure for runtestsf; the earlier attempt's evidence still remains in that run's logs.","title":"How Runtests uses the history"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/history/#Where-run-states-live","page":"Run states and history","text":"By default, run states live under runtests/runs/ in the Julia depot, in a directory for each project. Set RUNTESTS_RUNSTATE_DIR to use another directory. Several projects can share it. Runtests matches run states to a project by the UUID of the project. If there is no UUID, it uses the name of the project, then the name of the directory.","title":"Where run states live"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/history/#retention","page":"Run states and history","text":"Runtests keeps the newest 20 run states of this machine. It deletes an older run state, except when the deletion changes what the run states say about an item of the suite. These facts of an item keep a run state:\n\nthe latest verdict of the item, and when it was recorded,\nthe seed of a failure,\nthe newest duration and source hash of the item,\nwhether the newest run that selected the item stopped before the item.\n\nAn item that the suite no longer has stays on record, for a branch that still has it. It keeps no run state beyond the newest 20. The directory of a run, with its logs, is deleted with its run state, and only with it.\n\nRuntests.chores() also looks for deleted projects in the default run-state location. It removes this machine's run states after the newest file is at least 30 days old. A project in the system's temporary files has no 30-day wait. A project whose parent directory is missing or empty is left alone, as with a drive that is not mounted. Ordinary runs prune their own project's history; they do not sweep deleted projects. The sweep is disabled when RUNTESTS_RUNSTATE_DIR is set.","title":"Retention"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/history/#Run-states-of-other-machines","page":"Run states and history","text":"The name of the machine is RUNTESTS_HOST when it is set. In other cases, it is the name that macOS gives the machine, or the hostname on other systems. The macOS name stays the same when the hostname changes with the network.\n\nRetention leaves run states of other machines alone. A replay does not prune, and never deletes the run state that you give it.","title":"Run states of other machines"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/history/#Formats","page":"Run states and history","text":"Runtests reads only run states in its own format. A run warns once when its run state directory holds run states of another version of Runtests. Runtests does not use those run states, and does not delete them.","title":"Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/history/#Concurrent-runs","page":"Run states and history","text":"An editor, a REPL and CI jobs can share one run state directory. Each package record gets its own directory, and Runtests writes it to disk at least every 30 seconds. The run states of active runs are never pruned.\n\nA run is dead when its run state did not change for 90 seconds, or when its local process is known to have exited. A dead run counts as cancelled, and an item that was running in it counts as failing. A run whose setup fails finishes its run state as a run that ended before its first item. Thus no other run waits for it.\n\nMachines that share run states over a network must agree on the time to within a minute. Each RUNTESTS_HOST must name only one machine at a time.","title":"Concurrent runs"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/history/#Read-a-run-state","page":"Run states and history","text":"Runtests.read_run_state(path) reads a run state. path is the directory of a run or the run.runstate file in it. A truncated record, one left by a crashed run, or one written by another version gives as much as can be read, or nothing. Ordinary read and parse errors are suppressed so a broken old record does not stop a new run. An interrupt during parsing is rethrown; a file removed between reading and checking its modification time can cause a file-system error.","title":"Read a run state"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/measuring/#measuring","page":"Measure what each item costs","text":"A run can give you figures for each attempt at an item: its time, CPU time, compilation, garbage collection, allocations and memory. Give runtests or runtests_mono a function as on_item. The run calls the function in the process that started it.","title":"Measure what each item costs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/measuring/#Collect-the-figures","page":"Measure what each item costs","text":"The run calls the function when each attempt at an item starts, and again when the attempt ends. A retry is a new attempt, so it gets two calls of its own.\n\nevents = Runtests.ItemEvent[]\nRuntests.runtests(on_item = ev -> ev.kind === :finished && push!(events, ev))\nsort!(events; by = ev -> ev.cpu, rev = true)   # the items with the most CPU time first","title":"Collect the figures"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/measuring/#The-fields-of-an-ItemEvent","page":"Measure what each item costs","text":"Each call gets a Runtests.ItemEvent with these fields:\n\nField Meaning\nkind :started or :finished.\nname, file, line The item.\npackage, root The name and root directory of the item's package. Together with name, these distinguish items in a combined run.\nattempt 1 for the first attempt, 2 for the first retry, and so on.\nworker, pid The number of the worker that ran the attempt, as in w1 in the lines of the run, and the ID of its process.\ntime When the attempt started or ended, as time() gives it.\nstate :running when the attempt starts. When it ends, the outcome: :passed, :failed, :errored, :timedout, :skipped, :broken_chain or :cancelled.\nmeasured true when the item measured the figures below itself.\nelapsed Seconds that the attempt took.\ncpu Seconds of CPU time that the process used, in its own code and in the kernel, over all its threads.\ncompile, recompile Seconds of compilation, and the part of them that was recompilation.\ngc Seconds of garbage collection.\nallocated Bytes allocated.\nallocations The number of allocations.\nmaxrss The most memory that the process held in RAM up to this point, in bytes.\n\npid is 0 when no process ran the attempt. This happens when the run cannot start a worker, for example because the init of the profile fails. It also happens to an item of a chain whose worker stopped before the item ran. For that item, worker is 0 too.","title":"The fields of an ItemEvent"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/measuring/#What-the-figures-cover","page":"Measure what each item costs","text":"Only the second call has the figures. Each figure except elapsed is for the whole process. CPU, compilation, garbage collection and allocation counts cover the interval measured around the item. maxrss is different: it is the process's lifetime high-water mark, which an earlier item may have set. With workers = 0, the process is the one that called runtests, so the interval can also include work that the run itself does in that process.\n\nMeasured item time excludes the profile's test_end and the deferred artifact producers. Their work still contributes to the run's total time. The :finished event is delivered after the verdict is recorded and before artifact collection begins; it does not signal that evidence files are complete.\n\nSometimes the run decides the outcome, and the item measures nothing. Examples are a timeout, a worker that stopped, an item of a chain that cannot run, and a stopped run. Then measured is false, the figures are 0, and elapsed is the time that the attempt held its worker.","title":"What the figures cover"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/measuring/#How-the-run-calls-the-function","page":"Measure what each item costs","text":"The run makes one call at a time, from its own tasks, and waits for each call to return. Thus a slow function makes the run slow. If the function throws an error, the run reports the error once and does not call the function again. The tests continue. An attempt that is in progress when you interrupt the run can end without its second call.","title":"How the run calls the function"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#configuration","page":"Configure runs","text":"This page tells you how to configure the runs of a suite: the number of workers, the timeouts, the output, the order and the groups. The configuration reference lists every key with its default.","title":"Configure runs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#Where-the-configuration-comes-from","page":"Configure runs","text":"A run takes each configuration value from the first of these sources that sets it:\n\na keyword of runtests, for example runtests(workers = 4),\nan environment variable RUNTESTS_<KEY>, for example RUNTESTS_WORKERS=4,\nthe [run] table of test/TestItems.toml,\nthe default.\n\nThe header of the run names each environment variable that set a value. CI can thus set the configuration of a job without a change to the project.\n\nIn a combined workspace run, run-wide values come from the workspace root's file. Each package's other settings come from its own file, with any shared profile defaults from the workspace root. A keyword or environment variable still takes precedence for every package.\n\nRuntests.runtests(workers = 4, timeout = 600, retries = 1)\n\n# test/TestItems.toml\n[run]\nworkers = 4\ntimeout = 600\nretries = 1\nlogs = \"issues\"\n\nIn the file, write a symbol value as a TOML string, for example logs = \"issues\". An unknown key or an incorrect value is an error, and Runtests reports it before any item runs.","title":"Where the configuration comes from"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#The-configuration-file","page":"Configure runs","text":"test/TestItems.toml is optional. It has four tables:\n\n[run]\nworkers = \"auto\"       # or an integer\ntimeout = 600          # seconds for each attempt at an item\ninit_timeout = 120     # seconds for each `init` expression; the default is `timeout`\ntest_end_timeout = 60  # seconds for each `test_end` expression; the default is `timeout`\n\n[order]\nfirst = [\"smoke test\"]         # exact names of items in the suite\nlast  = [\"large simulation\"]\n\n[groups]\ndefault = \"!gpu\"               # what a run selects when nothing else selects\ngpu = \"gpu\"\nquick = \"fast && !slow\"\n\n[profiles.bounds]\njulia_args = [\"--check-bounds=yes\"]   # for items with `profile = :bounds`\n\n[profiles] declares worker profiles. Profiles and environments tells you how to use them.\n\nTo read another file in place of test/TestItems.toml, give the config keyword: runtests(config = \"ci.toml\"). The path is relative to the current directory, and the file must exist. Only the config keyword selects another file. No environment variable and no file next to it do that.","title":"The configuration file"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#Workers-and-threads","page":"Configure runs","text":"workers = \"auto\" is the default. It chooses the number of workers from the number of CPUs, the threads of each worker and an assumed 4 GiB for each worker. The maximum is 8. Give an integer to set the number yourself. workers = 0 runs ordinary items in the process that calls runtests; sandboxed items and items of a named profile still use workers. Run tests explains this mode.\n\nthreads is the --threads value of each worker. The default is \"2,1\": two threads in the default pool and one interactive thread. A worker with threads = \"auto\" takes every CPU.","title":"Workers and threads"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#Timeouts-and-retries","page":"Configure runs","text":"timeout is the number of seconds that each attempt at an item can take. The default is 1800. A worker that goes past the timeout is stopped, and the item is recorded as timed out. init_timeout and test_end_timeout limit the init and test_end expressions of a profile, and artifacts_timeout limits the producers of a failed attempt. Each of them is timeout by default.\n\nA timeout must be positive and at most 2,147,483,647 seconds. Runtests rounds a fraction up to whole seconds. Work in the calling process with workers = 0 cannot be forcibly timed out.\n\nretries gives each item more attempts after a failure, from 0 to 126. The timeout and retries keywords of an item replace the values of the run for that item.\n\nfailfast = true stops the run when an item or chain still fails after its allowed retries. item_failfast = true stops each attempt at its first failed test. Its default is the run's failfast value; set it separately when you want a failing attempt to finish its remaining assertions before the run stops.","title":"Timeouts and retries"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#Output","page":"Configure runs","text":"logs controls what the run prints of the output of the items:\n\nValue What the run prints\n:issues The output of each item that did not pass, after its DONE line. This is the default.\n:batched The output of every item, when the item ends.\n:eager The output of every item, while it runs. This mode does not save the output to per-item log files.\n:quiet No output of any item. The output stays in the log files of the run.\n\nAn interactive session with one worker or less uses :eager by default. When many workers print at the same time, their output mixes. Thus other runs use :issues by default.\n\nThese keys also control the output:\n\nverbose = true includes passing testsets in the result summary. Use logs = :batched to print passing-item output too.\nmonitor = false switches off the memory monitor and the progress line.\nmonitor_interval is the number of seconds between progress lines when the output is not a terminal. The default is 30.\nfull_stacktraces = true keeps the frames of Runtests in the stack traces of failures.\nfull_names = true prints long item names in full, in place of a unique prefix.\ntestset_name is the name of the testset that the run returns. The default is \"Runtests\".","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#Memory","page":"Configure runs","text":"memory_threshold is the fraction of the memory of the machine at which the run waits before it starts new items. The default is 0.9. The value must be greater than 0 and at most 1. How a run works explains what the run does when memory is low.","title":"Memory"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#Random-numbers","page":"Configure runs","text":"seed sets the seed of the run. Each item draws its random numbers from this seed combined with its own name. Without seed, the run chooses a random seed and prints it in its header. A run with the same seed draws the same numbers in each item, whatever ran before the item. Environment variables take a seed as text, for example RUNTESTS_SEED=0x1234.","title":"Random numbers"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#order","page":"Configure runs","text":"[order] names items that start before or after all others:\n\n[order]\nfirst = [\"smoke test\"]\nlast  = [\"large simulation\"]\n\nOn a run of the whole suite, [order] must name items that exist. A run of a part of the suite accepts names of items outside the selection.\n\nwarning: Warning\nDo not use [order] for items that must run one after the other. Items of different profiles or environments, and sandboxed items, can run at the same time. Use a chain for items that must run in sequence, on one worker.","title":"Order"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#groups","page":"Configure runs","text":"[groups] gives names to tag expressions, written as the tags keyword takes them:\n\n[groups]\ndefault = \"!gpu\"\ngpu = \"gpu\"\nquick = \"fast && !slow\"\n\nruntests(group = \"gpu\"), or RUNTESTS_GROUP=gpu, runs the items that the expression selects. The header of the run says which group ran and what selected it: the keyword, the variable, or the default when nothing else selected. RUNTESTS_GROUP and default apply only to a call that selects nothing else. A path, a line, name, tags or group in the call replaces them.\n\nTwo names are reserved:\n\nall is the whole suite. You cannot declare it.\ndefault is what a run selects when nothing else selects. It is the group with this name, or the whole suite when no group has this name.\n\nA name that differs from all or default only in case is an error. A group that [groups] does not declare is an error that lists the declared groups. An expression with a tag that no item carries is also an error. Runtests.chores() makes sure that every group is valid.\n\nA declared group, including a declared default, makes the run a selection of the suite. Thus [order] can name items outside it. With no other filters, group = \"all\" runs the whole suite, as does default when no group of that name is declared. Adding a path, name or tag filter still narrows the run. This distinction also matters for the retention of run states.","title":"Groups"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/configuration/#Values-that-only-a-keyword-sets","page":"Configure runs","text":"Some keywords of runtests are not configuration keys, so they cannot go in [run] or in an environment variable. These keywords are name, tags, group, dry_run, replay, config and on_item. RUNTESTS_GROUP is the exception: it selects a group when the call selects nothing else.","title":"Values that only a keyword sets"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/run/#how-a-run-works","page":"How a run works","text":"This page explains what happens between a call to runtests and its result. A run reads the suite, plans the order, builds the test environment and runs the items on workers.","title":"How a run works"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/run/#The-processes-of-a-run","page":"How a run works","text":"The process that calls runtests is the coordinator. The coordinator never runs test items, except with workers = 0. It starts the worker processes, gives each worker one item at a time, collects the results and prints the output. If a worker crashes, only that worker stops. The coordinator records the item as an error and continues.","title":"The processes of a run"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/run/#Reading-the-suite","page":"How a run works","text":"The coordinator finds test items by parsing the test files. It does not evaluate the files. Thus it knows the name, the location, the keywords and the imports of each item before any worker starts. Each run parses the whole suite, also when the selection is one item. These problems stop the run: a file that does not parse, a name that two items use, and a Julia file in no category. The run then throws a ScanFailure that lists every problem.\n\nFrom the imports of the selected items, the coordinator knows which setups they need. It precompiles these setups before it starts the workers.","title":"Reading the suite"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/run/#test-environment","page":"How a run works","text":"Under Pkg.test, Runtests uses the environment that Pkg already built. It also respects an already active test/Project.toml. Otherwise it builds the environment with TestEnv, except for the workspace case below. Thus the dependencies that your package declares only for tests can be imported in a test item. These are the dependencies in [extras] and [targets], or in test/Project.toml. When the run ends, the environment that was active before is active again.\n\nWhen a package and its test/Project.toml belong to the same outermost Julia workspace, Runtests activates the test project and instantiates it, as Pkg.test does. The workspace can list both projects directly, or the package can list its own test project. They share the workspace manifest, so TestEnv does not build a separate environment.\n\nRuntests keeps resolved environments for the session and rebuilds them when their input files, such as Project.toml or a manifest, change. Reusing an environment does not guarantee that all compilation is reusable: changed source or worker flags can require new compiled code. The items in an environment directory run in that environment, stacked over the test environment.","title":"The test environment"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/run/#plan","page":"How a run works","text":"Before it starts the items, the coordinator plans the order in which it gives them out. It uses the durations that earlier runs recorded. A run gives out the items in this order:\n\nthe items that [order] first names,\nsandboxed items, while each process is still new,\nitems that need a new result:\nitems whose latest result is a failure,\nitems that the newest run that selected them stopped before,\nitems that are new, or changed since they last ran,\nitems that set the length of the run: at least 3 seconds, and more than a quarter of the share of work of one worker,\nall other items, in file order,\nthe items that [order] last names.\n\nWithin step 3, new or edited items and failures from the newest run come first, then older failures from newest to oldest. Within each urgency level, the long items go first and the rest follow file order. A chain is scheduled together, at the position of its most urgent member.\n\nIn step 5, each worker goes through its own range of files. Thus neighboring items can use the code that the worker already compiled. A worker that finishes its range takes half of the longest range that is left. In the other steps, the next free worker takes the next item.\n\nEach start configuration that the items use has a pool of workers. A start configuration is what a worker gets when it starts: its flags, threads, variables, init, preferences and environment. Pools start in order of their estimated completion, the latest first. The estimate starts with the time that a worker needs to be ready. It adds the larger of two times: the pool's work shared among its workers, and its longest item or chain. An item that no run timed counts as the typical duration of an item, also in a chain with timed items.\n\nWhen a pool has no items left, a worker of that pool can restart for another pool. The restart must make that pool finish sooner. To find out, the run plays out the items that are left in the pool, in the order that the workers take them. Each item goes to the worker that is free first. The workers of the pool are free at once, and the new worker is free only after its cost: its start, and the compilation of what the other workers already compiled. An item or a chain goes to one worker whole. Thus a new worker that is ready after the other workers took the last items makes the pool finish no sooner. Of the pools that a restart makes finish sooner, the worker restarts for the pool that finishes last. During the run, an item that no run timed counts as the average item of its pool so far.\n\nThe dry run shows the plan. It plays out the dispatch of the run with the recorded durations, so it is as good as those durations are. The why here column says which rule placed an item, when the rule was not file order.","title":"The plan"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/run/#Short-names","page":"How a run works","text":"When a name is much longer than the others, the plan shortens it to r\"^…\", in the plan and in the RUN and DONE lines. The short form is a prefix that no other item name in the suite starts with. Thus you can give it as name = to select that item. full_names = true writes every name in full.","title":"Short names"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/run/#Workers","page":"How a run works","text":"A worker starts with the flags, threads, environment variables and preferences of its profile, in the environment of its pool. Then it runs the init of its profile, and after that one item at a time. After each item that returns a result other than skipped, it runs the test_end of its profile. A timed-out or crashed worker cannot run this hook.\n\nEach item runs in a new module. A worker can reuse compiled code from earlier items, though new method specializations or invalidations can still require compilation. Items on one worker also share the state of the process. Write test items explains what this means for a test.\n\nA worker that goes past the timeout of its item is stopped. The item is recorded as timed out, and the run starts a new worker in its place.","title":"Workers"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/run/#memory","page":"How a run works","text":"The coordinator watches the memory of the machine. When the memory in use goes over memory_threshold, the run does these steps:\n\nIt holds off on new items.\nIt collects garbage.\nOnly then, it restarts the largest worker, after the item that the worker runs is finished.\n\nThe run never stops a running item for memory, because the memory can belong to another program. The hold ends when the memory in use is two points below memory_threshold, and the run says when it ends. The hold has a limit. Thus memory pressure from another program slows a run down, but never stops it.","title":"Memory"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/run/#Runs-that-hang","page":"How a run works","text":"A run can go a long time without a finished item. The watchdog allows the larger of the largest item timeout plus the applicable init and test_end limits, or artifacts_timeout, then adds the longest memory hold and five minutes. Finishing artifact collection also counts as progress. If nothing finishes within that budget, the run stops as hung: it kills the workers, records running items as timed out, and runtests throws. An attempt whose verdict was already recorded before collection keeps that verdict.","title":"Runs that hang"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/run/#The-output","page":"How a run works","text":"The coordinator writes all the output. A worker writes the output of its items to a log file, except under logs = :eager. When an item ends, the coordinator prints its block as one piece. Thus the output of another worker never lands inside it. The output reference describes each line.","title":"The output"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/run/#The-record","page":"How a run works","text":"Each run writes a run state while it goes on, and prints its path at the end. The next run uses it to plan, and runtestsf uses it to find the failures. Run states and history explains what a run state records and which run states Runtests keeps.","title":"The record"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/syntax/#syntax","page":"Syntax","text":"This page gives the exact syntax of test files, test items, test templates and tag expressions. Write test items and Generate test items from a template explain how to use them.","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/syntax/#File-names","page":"Syntax","text":"Runtests reads the files under test/. Each Julia file there must match one of these rules:\n\nPath Meaning\ntest/runtests.jl The entry point that Pkg.test runs.\ntest/**/*_test.jl, test/**/*_tests.jl A test file. It holds @testitem declarations only.\ntest/testsetups/Name.jl, test/testsetups/Name/src/Name.jl A test setup: the module Name.\ntest/testtemplates/*_test_template.jl, test/testtemplates/*_tests_template.jl A test template. It holds @testtemplate declarations only.\ntest/testfixtures/** Input for the tests. Runtests never reads it as part of the suite.\ntest/**/ with a Project.toml or JuliaProject.toml An environment directory. Its test files run in that environment.\n\nA hidden file or directory, whose name starts with ., is not part of the suite. A Julia file that matches no rule stops the run.\n\nEvery nested Project.toml or JuliaProject.toml that discovery encounters makes its directory an environment directory. The innermost one that holds a test file is the environment of its items. The root test/ project is the test environment. Setup packages under test/testsetups/, projects under test/testtemplates/ and fixture projects under test/testfixtures/ are not environment directories. Only the suite-root test/testfixtures/ is reserved: a deeper directory with that name is an ordinary directory.\n\nThe expansion of test/testtemplates/x_tests_template.jl is test/x_tests.jl.","title":"File names"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/syntax/#@testitem","page":"Syntax","text":"@testitem \"name\" keyword=value ... begin\n    body\nend\n\nThe name is a string literal that is not blank. It must be unique across the suite. The keywords come between the name and begin, in any order.","title":"@testitem"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/syntax/#testitem-keywords","page":"Syntax","text":"Keyword Value Default Meaning\ntags A vector of symbols, for example [:fast, :math]. none The tags that a run can select by.\ntimeout A positive number of seconds. The timeout of the run. The time that each attempt can take.\nretries An integer from 0 to 126. The retries of the run. The number of more attempts after a failure. A chain retries from its first item.\nskip true, false, or an expression that gives a Bool. false Skip the item. The worker evaluates the expression before the body.\nfailfast true or false. The item_failfast of the run. Stop the item at its first failed test.\nchain A symbol, for example :db. none The items of a chain run in sequence, on one worker.\nsandbox true or false. false true runs the item alone in a new process.\nprofile The name of a profile as a symbol, for example :bounds. :default The profile of the workers that run the item.\n\nEach value except the value of skip must be a literal. Arithmetic on numeric literals is a literal too, for example timeout = 5*60. sandbox = true and chain cannot go together. sandbox = true and profile combine. All items of one chain must use the same profile and be in the same environment. sandbox = :name is an error that tells you to write profile = :name.\n\nThe skip expression runs on the worker, with Test and the package in scope, before the imports of the body. An exception in it, or a value that is not a Bool, is an error of the item.","title":"Keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/syntax/#The-body","page":"Syntax","text":"The body runs at the top level of a new module, with the soft scope of the REPL. Test and the package under test are in scope. The variables of the body are untyped globals.","title":"The body"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/syntax/#@testtemplate","page":"Syntax","text":"@testtemplate \"name with $x\" keyword=value ... for x in xs, y in ys\n    body with $x\nend\n\nA template makes one test item for each iteration of the loop. With several loop variables, the first one changes slowest. The loop runs in a module that holds Test, the package under test, and each package that the body imports, by its module name only.","title":"@testtemplate"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/syntax/#Interpolation","page":"Syntax","text":"Location Form Meaning\nName \"case $n\" The value of the iteration goes into the literal name.\nBody or keywords $n The value of the loop variable goes in as Julia code.\nKeywords $(expression) The expansion evaluates the expression once for each iteration, in the scope of the loop, and writes its value.\nBody $ before anything else than a loop variable The expansion leaves it as it is, for a macro of the body.\nQuoted expressions any $ The expansion leaves it as it is.\n\nAn inserted value is written with repr, as the module of the item sees it with the imports of the body. It must evaluate back to an equal value there. A loop variable without $ in the body is an error, unless the body binds it, as x = $x does. Inside a string in the body, write \"$($x)\" to insert the value.\n\n@testtemplate evaluated anywhere else than in a template that chores expands throws an error.","title":"Interpolation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/syntax/#tag-expressions","page":"Syntax","text":"The tags keyword of runtests and the values of [groups] take a tag expression as a string:\n\nexpression  = alternative { \"||\" alternative }\nalternative = term { \"&&\" term }\nterm        = [ \"!\" ] tag\ntag         = one or more characters that are not white space, \"!\", \"&\", \"|\", \":\", \"(\" or \")\"\n\n! negates one tag. && binds tighter than ||. Parentheses are not part of the syntax. White space around the operators has no effect. Examples:\n\nExpression Selects\n\"fast\" The items with the tag fast.\n\"!slow\" The items without the tag slow.\n\"fast && !slow\" The items with fast and without slow.\n\"juliac || serializer\" The items with juliac, or with serializer, or with both.\n\"a && b || c\" The items with a and b, or with c.\n\nThe tags keyword also takes a symbol, which selects the items with that tag. A vector of symbols selects the items with all of its tags. An expression that names a tag that no item of the suite carries is an error.","title":"Tag expressions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Get-started","page":"Get started","text":"In this tutorial, you give a small package a test suite that Runtests runs. You write test items, run them with Pkg.test, run them at the REPL, repair a failure, and share code between items.","title":"Get started"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Make-a-package","page":"Get started","text":"You need Julia 1.11 or later. Start Julia in an empty directory and make a package named Geometry:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.generate(\"Geometry\")\n\nReplace the contents of Geometry/src/Geometry.jl with this module:\n\nmodule Geometry\n\nexport circle_area, square_area\n\ncircle_area(r) = π * r^2\nsquare_area(s) = s^2\n\nend","title":"Make a package"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Add-Runtests-to-the-test-dependencies","page":"Get started","text":"The tests of Geometry get their own environment in Geometry/test/. Add Runtests to it:\n\ncd(\"Geometry\")\nPkg.activate(\"test\")\nPkg.add(\"Runtests\")\nPkg.activate(\".\")\n\nPkg writes test/Project.toml with Runtests under [deps]. Commit this file with the suite.","title":"Add Runtests to the test dependencies"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Write-the-entry-point-and-the-first-test-items","page":"Get started","text":"Pkg.test runs the file test/runtests.jl. Make that file call Runtests:\n\n# test/runtests.jl\nusing Runtests\nRuntests.runtests()\n\nTest items go in test files. The name of a test file ends in _test.jl or _tests.jl. Make test/area_tests.jl:\n\n# test/area_tests.jl\n@testitem \"circle area\" begin\n    @test circle_area(1) ≈ π\n    @test circle_area(2) ≈ 4π\nend\n\n@testitem \"square area\" tags=[:fast] begin\n    @test square_area(3) == 9\nend\n\nEach item gets Test and the package under test in its scope. Thus @test and circle_area work without using statements. A test file holds @testitem declarations and nothing else.","title":"Write the entry point and the first test items"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Run-the-suite-with-Pkg.test","page":"Get started","text":"Run the tests as for any package:\n\nPkg.test()\n\nThe output starts with a header block, then one line for each event of the run. The abbreviated output below illustrates the format; worker counts, assignments, paths, seeds and timings depend on your run.\n\n┌ [TEST] v0.1.0 · julia 1.13.3 · 2 test items in 1 file · seed 0xdd9c0263775e72e7 · 2 workers · threads 2,1\n│ env: /tmp/jl_4JWcNy/Project.toml\n└ startup: files 0.0s · plan 0.1s · setup 1.1s\n⚫ w1 · 14:54:35 · UP   · pid 45413 · threads 2,1\n🔵 w1 · 14:54:35 · RUN  · 1/2 · \"circle area\" · at test/area_tests.jl:1\n🟢 w1 · 14:54:36 · DONE · 1/2 · \"circle area\" · PASS ·  0.5s (11% compile) · maxrss 0.3 GiB\n┌ [TEST] ran 2 test items in 2.4s on 2 workers, all passed\n└ run state: ~/.julia/runtests/runs/Geometry-…/1793973275182436-45262.rundata\nTest Summary:        | Pass  Total  Time\nRuntests             |    3      3  2.4s\n  test/area_tests.jl |    3      3  0.5s\n\nThis example uses two workers. The last line of the closing block names the directory of the run. The directory holds the run state and the logs of the run. The output reference explains each part of the lines.","title":"Run the suite with Pkg.test"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Run-the-suite-at-the-REPL","page":"Get started","text":"At the REPL, you can run the suite without Pkg.test. For this, Runtests must be in an environment that Julia can load from, for example your default environment:\n\nPkg.activate()   # the default environment, for example @v1.12\nPkg.add(\"Runtests\")\nPkg.activate(\".\")\n\nWith the Geometry project active, load Runtests and run the suite:\n\nusing Runtests\nRuntests.runtests()\n\nRuntests prepares the test dependencies of Geometry itself. It keeps the resolved environment for the session and rebuilds it when its input files change.","title":"Run the suite at the REPL"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Repair-a-failure","page":"Get started","text":"Change the second test of \"circle area\" so that it fails:\n\n    @test circle_area(2) ≈ 2π\n\nRun the suite again with Runtests.runtests(). The failed item gets a block with its failure, directly after its DONE line. For example:\n\n🔴 w1 · 12:29:30 · DONE · 1/2 · \"circle area\" · FAIL ·  0.0s (94% compile) · maxrss 0.3 GiB\n┌ [1/2] FAIL \"circle area\"\n│ Test Failed at test/area_tests.jl:3\n│   Expression: circle_area(2) ≈ 2π\n│    Evaluated: 12.566370614359172 ≈ 6.283185307179586\n│ [ No captured logs\n└ @ test/area_tests.jl:1 on worker 1\n\nThe closing block tells you what to do next:\n\n└ run the failing items again: Runtests.runtestsf()\n\nOutside a @testset, a run with a failure throws an exception. Pkg.test then reports that the tests failed.\n\nChange the test back to 4π. Then run only the failed items:\n\nRuntests.runtestsf()\n\nRuntests reads the earlier run states to find the failing items, and runs \"circle area\" alone. It uses the same random seed that the item failed with. When no item is failing, runtestsf() says so and returns nothing:\n\n  [TEST] no test item is failing in the recorded runs","title":"Repair a failure"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Select-items","page":"Get started","text":"You can run a part of the suite. These calls select by tag, by name and by file:\n\nRuntests.runtests(tags = :fast)\nRuntests.runtests(name = \"circle area\")\nRuntests.runtests(\"test/area_tests.jl\")\n\nTo see the plan of a run without running it, give dry_run = true:\n\nRuntests.runtests(dry_run = true)\n\n  # · worker · test item     · at                   ·  est · tags\n  1 ·     w1 · \"circle area\" · test/area_tests.jl:1 · 0.5s\n  2 ·     w2 · \"square area\" · test/area_tests.jl:6 · 0.4s · fast\n\nThe est column shows each item's most recent recorded duration. The most recent run may not have selected every item. Run tests tells you all the ways to select items.","title":"Select items"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Share-code-between-items","page":"Get started","text":"Code that several items use goes in a test setup. A test setup is a module in test/testsetups/. Make test/testsetups/ShapeData.jl:\n\n# test/testsetups/ShapeData.jl\nmodule ShapeData\nexport RADII\nconst RADII = (0.5, 1, 2)\nend\n\nAdd an item to test/area_tests.jl that loads the setup with using:\n\n@testitem \"areas are positive\" begin\n    using ShapeData\n    for r in RADII\n        @test circle_area(r) > 0\n    end\nend\n\nRun the suite. Runtests finds the using ShapeData statement, and precompiles the setup before it starts the workers.","title":"Share code between items"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Let-chores-maintain-the-suite","page":"Get started","text":"Runtests.chores() does the maintenance work that a suite needs. Run it now:\n\nRuntests.chores()\n\n┌ [TEST] chores\n│ setups: 1 of 1 changed:\n│   `ShapeData`: moved to ShapeData/src/ShapeData.jl · deps: none\n│ templates: none\n│ test items: 3 in 1 file, all valid\n│ config: none\n│ run states: 5 in ~/.julia/runtests/runs/Geometry-…, none to delete\n│ checkouts that are gone: none with run states to delete\n└ done: 1\n\nchores converted ShapeData into a package with its own Project.toml. A setup that is a package gets its own precompile cache for each checkout. Commit the files that chores writes.","title":"Let chores maintain the suite"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Next-steps","page":"Get started","text":"You now have a suite that Runtests runs. These pages continue from here:\n\nWrite test items explains the keywords of @testitem, for example timeout, retries and skip.\nConfigure runs explains test/TestItems.toml.\nRun on CI explains how to keep the history of runs between CI jobs.\nGenerate test items from a template explains how one loop makes many items.\nRun a monorepo explains how packages in one Julia workspace share a run and workers.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/output/#output","page":"Output","text":"This page describes the output of a run, in five parts. These are the opening block, the lines of the run, the block of a failed item, the closing block and the dry-run plan. The examples come from a small suite. Times and memory depend on the suite and on the machine.","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/output/#The-opening-block","page":"Output","text":"┌ [TEST] v0.1.0 · julia 1.13.3 · 6 test items in 2 files · seed 0x522fb3b30334cd75 · 2 workers · threads 2,1\n│ env: /var/folders/…/jl_7AEUWb/Project.toml\n└ startup: files 0.0s · plan 0.1s · setup 1.2s\n\nThe first line gives the versions of Runtests and Julia, and the number of selected items and files. It also gives the seed, the number of workers and their threads. It names these values too, when they apply:\n\nthe selection, for example matching tags = [:fast],\nthe group and what selected it,\nthe Julia flags of the workers that a plain julia does not have, for example --check-bounds=yes -O1,\nthe environment variables that set configuration values,\nthe source of the coverage value.\n\nThe env line is the path of the test environment. The startup line gives the time to read the files, to make the plan and to set up the environment.","title":"The opening block"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/output/#The-lines-of-the-run","page":"Output","text":"Workers, items and the run itself each print lines of one shape:\n\n⚪ w0 · 14:52:16 · INFO · 0/6 · 0 failed · 0/2 workers · tree mem 920M (max 920M) · child max 449M · mem 82% · cpu  5.5/18 · testing 0s\n⚫ w1 · 14:52:16 · UP   · pid 35898 · threads 2,1\n🔵 w1 · 14:52:16 · RUN  · 1/6 · \"slow thing\" · at test/sub/b_test.jl:9\n🟢 w1 · 14:52:16 · DONE · 1/6 · \"slow thing\" · PASS ·  0.0s (94% compile) · maxrss 0.3 GiB\n⚫ w1 · 14:52:16 · EXIT · pid 35898 · 3 items · 0.3s\n\nEach line starts with a mark, the worker and the time. The worker is w1, w2 and so on, and w0 is the coordinator. The marks have these meanings:\n\nMark Meaning\n⚫ A worker starts (UP) or ends (EXIT).\n🔵 An item starts (RUN).\n🟢 An item passed.\n🔴 An item failed, errored or timed out.\n🟡 An item was skipped or was never reached.\n⚪ The progress line of the run (INFO).\n\nAn UP line gives the process ID and the threads of the worker. It also says sandbox for the process of a sandboxed item, and gives the Julia flags of its profile. When an item of the run uses a profile other than default, the line names the profile of every worker. In a combined run, the line names the profile with its package, for example profile default of B. There, only the default profile of the first package has no name on the line. That package is the workspace root when the root has tests. When the run uses an environment directory, the line names the environment of the worker.\n\nA RUN line gives the position of the item in the plan, its name and its location. A DONE line gives the outcome, the time, the fraction of the time that was compilation, and the most memory that the worker held.","title":"The lines of the run"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/output/#The-progress-line","page":"Output","text":"On a terminal, the progress line stays at the bottom, and the run draws it again as it goes. In other output, the run prints it at these times:\n\nevery monitor_interval seconds,\nafter every 10 finished items, but no sooner than 5 seconds after the last progress line,\nwhen the run goes from setup to testing,\nwhen memory use crosses one of the high-memory marks, starting at 90%.\n\nThe progress line gives these values:\n\nthe finished items and the selected items,\nthe number of failed items,\nthe busy workers and all workers,\nthe memory of the processes of the run, and of the largest of them,\nthe memory and CPU use of the machine,\nthe stage of the run and its time.","title":"The progress line"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/output/#The-block-of-a-failed-item","page":"Output","text":"An item that did not pass gets a block with its results, right after its DONE line:\n\n🔴 w1 · 12:55:14 · DONE · 1/1 · \"fails\" · FAIL ·  0.0s (92% compile) · maxrss 0.3 GiB\n┌ [1/1] FAIL \"fails\"\n│ Test Failed at test/faults_test.jl:6\n│   Expression: 1 == 2\n│ [ No captured logs\n└ @ test/faults_test.jl:5 on worker 1\n\nThe block holds the failed tests, the output of the item under Captured logs, and the path of the files that the item kept. The last line gives the location of the item and the worker. The run prints the block as one piece, so the output of another worker never lands inside it. Under logs = :quiet, the block has no output of the item.","title":"The block of a failed item"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/output/#The-closing-block","page":"Output","text":"┌ [TEST] ran 6 test items in 2.1s on 2 workers, all passed\n│ setup   · 1.1s · tree max  445M · child max  445M · coordinator\n│ testing · 0.9s · tree max  1.0G · child max  460M · coordinator + 2 workers · 85% compile\n│ (summed resident sizes over-count pages the processes share)\n│ machine · 52.9G of 64.0G in use at peak\n│ cpu · 8% of 18 threads for this run's processes, 24% for the whole machine (averages over the run)\n└ run state: ~/.julia/runtests/runs/MyPackage-8db4f545/1790247276123456-96211.rundata\n\nThe first line gives the number of items, the time, the workers and the outcome. Then comes one line for each stage of the run, with its time and memory:\n\ntree max is the peak of the summed memory of every process that the run owns. These are the coordinator, the workers, and the processes that they start, for example Pkg or a process that a test starts.\nchild max is the largest single process.\nThe list after it says which processes the peak covers, for example coordinator + 8 workers + 3 spawned.\n\nThe cpu line gives two averages over the run, each a fraction of the CPU threads of the machine. The first is the use by the processes of the run. The second is the use by the whole machine. The difference shows how much CPU time other programs used. On Windows, only the use by the whole machine is given, because Windows keeps no total for the children of a process.\n\nThe block ends with these lines, when they apply:\n\ncoverage, with the fraction of lines that ran,\nrun state, the directory of the run, which holds the run state and the logs,\nin a combined run, one line for each package in place of run state, with the items of the package, those that did not pass, and the directory of its run,\ncopied to, the directory that RUNTESTS_ARTIFACT_DIR named,\nthe evidence that failed attempts kept,\nrun the failing items again, with the command to type, when items failed.\n\nAfter the block, Test prints its usual summary.","title":"The closing block"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/output/#The-plan-of-a-dry-run","page":"Output","text":"┌ [TEST] dry run · v0.1.0 · julia 1.13.3 · 6 test items in 2 files · 2 workers · threads 2,1\n│ startup: files 0.0s\n│ setups: `BasicSetup`\n│ timeout: 1800s · retries: 0 · failfast: false · logs: issues · memory_threshold: 0.9\n│ estimated work: w1 0.049s · w2 0.058s\n└ order and workers: predicted from the recorded durations, an item without one counting as a typical one\n\n  # · worker · test item    · at                   ·  est · tags       · why here      · details\n  1 ·     w1 · \"slow thing\" · test/sub/b_test.jl:9 · 0.0s · slow       · [order] first · timeout 120s\n  2 ·     w2 · \"chain one\"  · test/sub/b_test.jl:1 · 0.0s ·            ·               · chain `seq` 1/2\n  3 ·     w2 · \"chain two\"  · test/sub/b_test.jl:5 · 0.0s ·            ·               · chain `seq` 2/2\n  4 ·     w1 · \"add works\"  · test/a_test.jl:1     · 0.0s · fast\n  5 ·     w2 · \"uses setup\" · test/a_test.jl:11    · 0.0s ·            ·               · setup `BasicSetup`\n  6 ·     w1 · \"mul works\"  · test/a_test.jl:6     · 0.0s · fast, math\n\nThe table lists the items in the expected order of the run. Each row also gives the worker that is expected to run the item. The estimated work line gives the time that each worker is expected to be busy, from the recorded durations. Without recorded durations, the plan treats all items as equal, and the block has no estimated work line and no est column. The columns have these meanings:\n\nColumn Meaning\n# The position in the plan.\nworker The worker that is expected to run the item.\ntest item The name, or a unique prefix of a long name.\nat The file and the line of the item.\nest The newest recorded duration, in seconds with one decimal. The column shows when earlier runs recorded durations.\ntags The tags of the item.\nwhy here The rule that placed the item, when the rule is not file order.\ndetails The keywords and setups of the item.","title":"The plan of a dry run"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/output/#The-output-of-chores","page":"Output","text":"Runtests.chores() prints one block, with a line for each step. A step that takes longer than 3 seconds shows its duration on its line. Maintain the suite shows an example.\n\nRuntests.chores_mono() prints one block for all packages of a monorepo, with paths from the workspace root. The block also shows the groups of workers. Run the suites of a monorepo describes it.","title":"The output of chores"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/ci/#ci","page":"Run on CI","text":"This page tells you how to run a suite on CI and how to split it over jobs. It also tells you how to keep the history of runs between jobs. The examples use GitHub Actions.","title":"Run on CI"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/ci/#Run-the-tests","page":"Run on CI","text":"On CI, run the tests with Pkg.test, as for any package. julia-actions/julia-runtest does this:\n\n- uses: julia-actions/setup-julia@v2\n- uses: julia-actions/cache@v2\n- uses: julia-actions/julia-buildpkg@v1\n- uses: julia-actions/julia-runtest@v1\n\nOutside a terminal, the run prints a progress line every monitor_interval seconds, 30 by default. It also prints one after every 10 finished items, but no sooner than 5 seconds after the last one.","title":"Run the tests"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/ci/#Configure-a-job-with-environment-variables","page":"Run on CI","text":"Each run setting can come from an environment variable named RUNTESTS_ and the key in capitals. A job sets its configuration in this way, without a change to the project:\n\n- uses: julia-actions/julia-runtest@v1\n  env:\n    RUNTESTS_WORKERS: 4\n    RUNTESTS_RETRIES: 1\n\nA keyword of runtests replaces the variable, and the variable replaces test/TestItems.toml. The header of the run names each variable that set a value. The configuration reference lists all variables.","title":"Configure a job with environment variables"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/ci/#Split-the-suite-over-jobs","page":"Run on CI","text":"Declare groups in test/TestItems.toml, and give each job of the matrix a group in RUNTESTS_GROUP:\n\nstrategy:\n  matrix:\n    group: [default, gpu]\nsteps:\n  - uses: julia-actions/julia-runtest@v1\n    env:\n      RUNTESTS_GROUP: ${{ matrix.group }}\n\nRUNTESTS_GROUP applies only when runtests selects nothing else. An empty RUNTESTS_GROUP counts as unset. Thus a job whose matrix.group is empty runs what a run selects by default.","title":"Split the suite over jobs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/ci/#ci-history","page":"Run on CI","text":"Runtests plans each run from the run states of earlier runs. On CI, each job starts on a new machine, so the job must restore the run states of earlier jobs. Cache the run states between jobs. Upload the run states and logs of each job as an artifact.\n\nEach run keeps its run state and its logs in a directory of its own, <run>.rundata. The run state is the file run.runstate in that directory.\n\nThis example is for a matrix with os, version and group:\n\n# Restore the run states that the last job with this os, version and group saved.\n- uses: actions/cache/restore@v4\n  with:\n    # The run states only, without their logs. The save step must name the same path.\n    path: ${{ runner.temp }}/runtests/*.rundata/run.runstate\n    # No entry has this key yet. The restore takes the newest entry whose key starts with restore-keys.\n    key: runtests-${{ matrix.os }}-${{ matrix.version }}-${{ matrix.group }}-${{ github.run_id }}-${{ github.run_attempt }}\n    restore-keys: runtests-${{ matrix.os }}-${{ matrix.version }}-${{ matrix.group }}-\n- uses: julia-actions/julia-runtest@v1\n  env:\n    # The group of test items that this job runs.\n    RUNTESTS_GROUP: ${{ matrix.group }}\n    # Where the run reads the restored run states and writes its own.\n    RUNTESTS_RUNSTATE_DIR: ${{ runner.temp }}/runtests\n    # A stable machine name, so that the run also prunes the restored run states.\n    RUNTESTS_HOST: ci-${{ matrix.os }}-${{ matrix.version }}\n    # Where the run copies its own directory when it ends, for the upload step.\n    RUNTESTS_ARTIFACT_DIR: ${{ runner.temp }}/runtests-artifact\n# Save the run states for the next job, also when the tests fail.\n- uses: actions/cache/save@v4\n  if: always()\n  with:\n    path: ${{ runner.temp }}/runtests/*.rundata/run.runstate\n    key: runtests-${{ matrix.os }}-${{ matrix.version }}-${{ matrix.group }}-${{ github.run_id }}-${{ github.run_attempt }}\n# When the job fails, upload this run's directory: its run state, logs and failure evidence.\n- uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4\n  if: failure()\n  with:\n    name: runtests-${{ matrix.os }}-${{ matrix.version }}-${{ matrix.group }}\n    path: ${{ runner.temp }}/runtests-artifact\n\nThe example uses these variables:\n\nRUNTESTS_RUNSTATE_DIR is the directory that holds the directories of the runs.\nRUNTESTS_HOST is the name of the machine in the run states.\nRUNTESTS_ARTIFACT_DIR is a directory into which each run copies its own directory when it ends. The run also copies it after a failure or an interrupt.","title":"Keep the history of runs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/ci/#The-cache","page":"Run on CI","text":"The cache holds the run states and not the logs, because the next job plans from the run states only. Use these rules for the cache:\n\nName the run states with *.rundata/run.runstate in path. The cache then holds each run's directory with the run state only.\nGive the restore step and the save step the same path. The cache finds an entry by its key and by its paths. A restore with other paths does not find the entry.\nUse a unique key for each run, and restore-keys to get the most recent cache.\nSave the cache also when the tests fail, with if: always(). Then the next job sees those failures.\nPut each dimension of the matrix in the key. Two jobs of one workflow run with the same key both save, and the cache keeps only one of them.\n\nwarning: Warning\nDo not name the directories, *.rundata, in path with a pattern such as !**/*.log to exclude the logs. The cache archives a directory as a whole, and the exclusion has no effect.\n\nIn a monorepo, each job can test one package with the project input of julia-runtest. Then add the package to the key and to the name of the artifact, as the example adds the group. To run the combined suites in one job, launch Julia from the workspace root with a project whose [deps] declares Runtests:\n\n- run: julia --project=lib/A/test -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate(); using Runtests; Runtests.runtests_mono()'\n\nA combined run writes one run state per selected package. The cache path above covers those run states when RUNTESTS_RUNSTATE_DIR is shared; RUNTESTS_ARTIFACT_DIR receives one subdirectory per package, each with that package's run directory and logs. A root package can instead use Pkg.test with a test/runtests.jl that calls runtests_mono, as the monorepo guide shows.","title":"The cache"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/ci/#The-machine-name","page":"Run on CI","text":"A stable RUNTESTS_HOST makes the restored run states part of the normal retention. Without it, each job has a new hostname, and run states of other machines collect. On CI without RUNTESTS_HOST, a run warns when its directory holds run states of other machines.","title":"The machine name"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/ci/#ci-artifact","page":"Run on CI","text":"The artifact holds the directory of this job's run: its run state, its logs and its failure evidence. It holds none of the history. On your machine, run the failures of a downloaded run again:\n\nRuntests.runtestsf(replay = \"path/to/the.rundata\")\n\nWhen items fail, the closing block of the run prints this command with the name of the copied directory.\n\nif: failure() uploads nothing when a retry passes. To also keep the evidence of a failed attempt that a later attempt repaired, use if: always().","title":"The artifact"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/ci/#Coverage-on-CI","page":"Run on CI","text":"Pkg.test(coverage = true) works with Runtests, and julia-runtest uses it by default. Runtests can also merge the counts of the workers into one lcov.info file. Measure coverage tells you about both ways.","title":"Coverage on CI"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/ci/#ci-templates","page":"Run on CI","text":"To make sure that the committed expansions agree with the current data, run chores and check for changes under test/. The Julia environment used by this step must already be instantiated and contain Runtests. This example uses test/Project.toml; use the environment where your project makes Runtests available if it has no test project:\n\n- run: julia --project=test -e 'using Runtests; Runtests.chores()'\n- name: Check generated test files\n  shell: bash\n  run: |\n    changes=$(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all -- test/)\n    if [ -n \"$changes\" ]; then\n      printf '%s\\n' \"$changes\"\n      exit 1\n    fi\n\nThe second step fails for modified, deleted or new untracked files under test/. Unlike git diff, it also catches a newly generated expansion that was never committed. Generated test files must not be ignored by git. chores can also update setup packages, so commit those changes together with the expansions.","title":"Template expansions on CI"},{"category":"section","location":"glossary/#glossary","page":"Glossary","text":"This page defines the terms of this manual, in alphabetical order.\n\nTerm Definition\nattempt One execution of a test item. A retry is a new attempt.\nchain A set of test items with the same chain keyword. They run in sequence, on one worker.\ncombined run One call to runtests_mono. It can run one or more packages of a workspace in one plan.\ncoordinator The process that calls runtests or runtests_mono. It plans the run, starts the workers and prints the output.\ndry run A run with dry_run = true. It prints the plan and runs nothing.\nenvironment directory A directory under test/ with a Project.toml or JuliaProject.toml of its own. The test files in it run in that environment.\nevidence The files that a failed attempt keeps: the outputs in artifacts_dir() and the files of its producers.\nexpansion The test file that chores writes from a test template.\ngroup A named tag expression in [groups] of test/TestItems.toml.\nplan The order in which a run gives out its items, and the worker slot expected to run each item.\nproducer A function that an item registers with on_failure. It writes evidence after a failed attempt.\nprofile A named worker configuration in [profiles]: flags, threads, variables, expressions and preferences. An item chooses it with profile = :name.\nreplay A run of the items of a recorded run state, with its configuration and seeds.\nretention The rules that decide which run states Runtests keeps.\nrun One call to runtests, runtestsf or runtests_mono.\nrun state A binary file that records a suite's part of a run: its items, results, configuration and seeds.\nsandbox An item with sandbox = true runs alone in a new process, under its profile.\nseed The number from which a run derives the random numbers of each item.\ntag A symbol in the tags keyword of an item. A run can select items by tag.\ntag expression A string such as \"fast && !slow\" that selects items by their tags.\ntest environment The Julia environment in which the items run. It holds the package and its test dependencies.\ntest file A file under test/ whose name ends in _test.jl or _tests.jl. It holds @testitem declarations.\ntest fixture Input for tests in test/testfixtures/. Runtests never reads it as part of the suite.\ntest item A named block of tests, declared with @testitem. It runs alone, in a module of its own.\ntest setup A module in test/testsetups/ that test items load with using.\ntest template A file in test/testtemplates/ of @testtemplate loops. chores expands it into a test file.\nunion environment The environment of the shared workers of a combined run. It holds the test dependencies of all its packages that are not isolated.\nverdict The latest recorded pass or failure of an item. Failures include errors, timeouts and lost-worker outcomes. A skip or explicit cancellation leaves the previous verdict unchanged. See Run states and history.\nworker A Julia process that a run starts to run test items.","title":"Glossary"},{"category":"section","location":"#Runtests.jl","page":"Home","text":"Runtests.jl runs the tests of a Julia package as independent test items on several worker processes. A test item is a named block of tests that runs in its own module. A worker is a Julia process that a run starts to run test items.\n\n@testitem \"adds numbers\" tags=[:fast] begin\n    @test 1 + 1 == 2\nend","title":"Runtests.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-a-run-does","page":"Home","text":"Each call to Runtests.runtests() does these steps:\n\nIt finds the test items by parsing the test files. It does not evaluate the files to find them.\nIt uses a suitable active test environment or prepares one. Pkg.test() supplies one, but you can also call Runtests.runtests() directly.\nIt chooses a number of workers from the CPUs and the memory of the machine.\nIt plans the order from earlier runs. Recent failures and long items start first. Other items run in file order, so that a worker can use code that it already compiled.\nIt records the run in a run state file. A run state file holds the items, the results, the configuration and the random seeds of one run.\n\nWith the run state, you can run the failures again with Runtests.runtestsf(), or replay a recorded run with the same seeds. A recorded run can come from your machine or from CI.","title":"What a run does"},{"category":"section","location":"#Install","page":"Home","text":"Runtests requires Julia 1.11 or later. Add it to the test dependencies of your package. If your package has a test/Project.toml, add Runtests to that environment:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"test\")\nPkg.add(\"Runtests\")\n\nIf your package declares its test dependencies under [extras] and [targets] in Project.toml, add Runtests there. Keep the dependencies and the test targets that are already in the file:\n\n[extras]\nRuntests = \"632efd68-cd41-4e05-bd25-b86dc2150078\"\nTest = \"8dfed614-e22c-5e08-85e1-65c5234f0b40\"\n\n[targets]\ntest = [\"Test\", \"Runtests\"]\n\nIn both cases, give Runtests a compat bound, for example Runtests = \"0.1\" under [compat].\n\nTo use Runtests at the REPL for any package, also add it to your default environment, for example @v1.12. The tutorial shows both uses.","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-this-manual-is-organized","page":"Home","text":"The manual has four parts. Each part answers a different kind of question.\n\nPart Use it to\nGet started Convert a small package to Runtests, step by step.\nGuides Do one task, for example select the items to run, share code between items, run a monorepo or run on CI.\nExplanation Learn how a run works and how Runtests keeps history.\nReference Look up the syntax, every configuration key, the output, the files, the editor protocol and the API.\n\nThe glossary defines the terms that the manual uses.","title":"How this manual is organized"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/repl/#repl","page":"Work at the REPL","text":"This page tells you how to work with a suite at the REPL. You can make the session look like a worker, run a pasted test item, and step into an item with a debugger.","title":"Work at the REPL"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/repl/#Make-the-session-look-like-a-worker","page":"Work at the REPL","text":"Runtests.activate()        # or Runtests.activate(\"path/to/Package\")\nRuntests.deactivate()\n\nactivate makes the session look like the worker of a test item. It activates the test environment of the package and puts test/testsetups/ on LOAD_PATH. Then using MySetups and the test-only dependencies of the package work as they do inside an item. activate returns the path of the environment. deactivate restores the environment that was active and the LOAD_PATH. Runtests.is_activated() tells you whether activate is in force.\n\nFor the shared packages of a workspace, use Runtests.activate_mono() after loading Runtests from a project in that workspace. It activates the combined test environment and the shared setups. A pasted item belongs to the package that holds the REPL's current directory; an item brought in with include belongs to the package that holds its file. In either case its body runs in that package's root:\n\nRuntests.activate_mono()\ncd(\"lib/A\")                 # from the workspace root, before pasting an A item\n# paste an @testitem from A here\nRuntests.deactivate()\n\ndeactivate restores the project and LOAD_PATH, but leaves the directory changed by cd alone. From the workspace root, use Runtests.activate(\"lib/Legacy\") for an isolated package instead; activate_mono reports an error if an item belongs to one.","title":"Make the session look like a worker"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/repl/#Run-a-pasted-test-item","page":"Work at the REPL","text":"A @testitem that you paste into the REPL runs at once:\n\njulia> @testitem \"adds numbers\" begin\n           @test 1 + 1 == 2\n       end\n🔵 16:30:28 · RUN  · \"adds numbers\" · at REPL[2]:1\nTest Summary: | Pass  Total  Time\nadds numbers  |    1      1  0.0s\n🟢 16:30:28 · DONE · \"adds numbers\" · PASS ·  0.0s (96% compile) · maxrss 0.6 GiB\n\nThe same parser reads it as reads a test file. Thus a keyword that is an error in a file is an error at the REPL too. The item runs in the same way as in a run. It runs in a new module, with soft scope, and with Test and the package in scope. An include of a test file runs its items in the same way.\n\nThe keywords behave as follows at the REPL:\n\nskip and failfast apply.\nsandbox and profile apply. Runtests starts a worker for the item, with the profile and the environment that a run gives it. Without profile, the worker uses [profiles.default]. The worker gets the flags, the environment variables, the preferences, the init and the test_end of the profile. A failure in test_end counts.\nAn item from a file in an environment directory also gets a worker, in that environment.\ntimeout, retries and chain need a run around the item. Runtests ignores them with a warning. An item with a worker of its own is the exception: it keeps its timeout and retries.\ntags have no effect.\n\nAn item running in this process, and its artifact producers, have no enforceable timeout. A sandboxed item keeps its explicitly declared timeout and retries; it does not inherit those two limits from [run]. Its profile hooks and artifact producers use their configured limits. Investigate failures explains where a pasted item's evidence is kept.","title":"Run a pasted test item"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/repl/#debug","page":"Work at the REPL","text":"With Debugger.jl loaded, Runtests.debug steps into one test item in the current process:\n\njulia> using Debugger\n\njulia> Runtests.debug()                        # the most recent failure\n\njulia> Runtests.debug(\"adds numbers\")          # an exact name\n\njulia> Runtests.debug(r\"^adds\")                # the one item whose name matches\n\njulia> Runtests.debug(\"test/math_test.jl:12\")  # the item that contains line 12\n\ndebug steps into exactly one item. An exact name, a Regex or a path selects it. Runtests reads a path as runtests reads one, relative to the current directory. The path is a file.jl:line, or a file or directory that holds one item. A selection that gives no item, or several items, is an error that lists what it found.","title":"Step into an item with a debugger"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/repl/#Without-a-name","page":"Work at the REPL","text":"Without a name, debug steps into the most recent recorded failure, error or timeout among the failing items, with the seed that it failed with. It also names the other failures it can step into. If no such failure is recorded, debug() throws NoTestsError; name an item to inspect it anyway. A failing item that you name also runs with the seed that it failed with. Give seed to use another seed. As with a rerun, the seed does not restore external state or task scheduling.","title":"Without a name"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/repl/#What-the-item-gets","page":"Work at the REPL","text":"The item gets what a run gives it. It gets its module and imports, its environment, the setups, and the env, init and test_end of its profile. The body of the item becomes a function, and the debugger enters it at the first call, in the test file. Code that cannot be part of a function ran before then. Examples are using, struct, const and a method of Base.show.\n\nOnly that item runs, not the items before it in a chain. Before the item starts, debug lists what this process cannot give it. Examples are the julia_args of a profile and sandbox = true. The item, its hooks and its artifact producers run in the current process without enforceable timeouts. An item that you leave in the debugger before it ends is recorded as an error, not as a pass.\n\ndebug returns the testset of the item.","title":"What the item gets"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#editor-protocol","page":"Editor protocol","text":"An editor drives a Runtests server over the standard streams of the server process. The editor writes commands to stdin and reads events from stdout, one JSON object on each line in each direction. Everything written for people goes to stderr, which an editor can show as a log. This includes the lines of the run, the output of Pkg and the output of the test items.","title":"Editor protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#Start-the-server","page":"Editor protocol","text":"julia --project=<an environment with Runtests> -e 'using Runtests; Runtests.serve(\"<package>\")'\n\nRuntests.serve(\"<package>\"; config = \"<file>\") makes every listing and run of the session read <file> in place of test/TestItems.toml. A relative path is relative to the directory in which the server starts.\n\nserve takes over the stdout of the process. After it starts, anything else written to stdout goes to stderr, from Julia or from C. Thus a stray println cannot get into the stream of events. serve returns when stdin ends or a shutdown command arrives. It cancels a run in progress first. One server serves one package, and runs one run at a time.\n\nRuntests.serve_mono(\"<workspace root>\") serves the packages of a workspace, as runtests_mono runs them. Its listing holds the items of every package, and each run is a combined run. Two items of two packages can have the same name, so an item is known by its key: the name of its package and its own name.\n\nserve_mono reads each package's own configuration file and has no config argument. The protocol version is 3, and the hello event gives it. An editor should check that version before sending a selection: earlier versions use different item identifiers. A change that can break an editor written against the protocol increases the version.","title":"Start the server"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#Commands","page":"Editor protocol","text":"Every command is an object with a command field. It can have an id of any JSON value. Replies echo a non-null id when the command could be read; an absent or null ID is omitted from replies. Events for a run keep the ID of its run command.\n\nCommand Fields Answer\nlist none items, or error.\nrun items: item keys, each an object with package and name. names: exact item names for serve only; serve_mono rejects it. Without either selection, the run uses its default selection. options: an object of configuration values. run_started, then item_started and item_finished for each attempt, then run_finished. Or error.\ncancel none No separate acknowledgement: the run ends with run_finished, state cancelled, and the original run ID. An error when nothing runs carries the cancel ID.\nshutdown none bye, without an ID.","title":"Commands"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#The-options-field","page":"Editor protocol","text":"options takes these keywords of runtests, with the same meaning and the same validation:\n\nKeywords Value\nworkers, threads, retries, memory_threshold As for runtests.\ntimeout, init_timeout, test_end_timeout Seconds.\nfailfast, item_failfast, verbose, full_stacktraces, coverage true or false.\nlogs A string, for example \"batched\".\n\nThese keys also apply:\n\nseed is an integer, or the \"0x…\" text that run_started gives. JSON integers stop at 2^63, so the text form can hold every seed.\ngroup is a group of [groups], or \"all\" for the whole suite.\nreplay is the path of a run state to run again.\n\nOther run settings come from the configuration file and environment variables, as for any run. For example, artifacts_timeout is set in [run] or through RUNTESTS_ARTIFACTS_TIMEOUT; it is not accepted in options.","title":"The options field"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#Selection","page":"Editor protocol","text":"An editor selects files, folders or tags by sending the keys of the items in them, from its last list. run_started lists the items that the run selected. The editor compares this list with what it asked for. A key that the suite does not have goes into the unknown field of run_started, and the other items still run. When none of the keys exists, the answer is an error.","title":"Selection"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#Events","page":"Editor protocol","text":"Every event is an object with an event field. Replies carry IDs as described above; hello and bye have no ID. The server writes one event on each line of stdout. The first event is hello, and the last event is bye.","title":"Events"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#hello","page":"Editor protocol","text":"The first line, before any command. Its fields are protocol, the runtests and julia versions, the pid of the server, the root of the package or workspace, config, combined and packages. config is the file that the server reads in place of test/TestItems.toml, or null. combined is true for serve_mono. packages holds the name and root of each package that the server serves.","title":"hello"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#items","page":"Editor protocol","text":"The answer to list. Its fields are root, testdir, packages, items, errors and failed. testdir is null for serve_mono. packages holds the name, root and testdir of each package. failed holds the keys of the items that are failing, which are the items that Runtests.runtestsf() runs in their package. The last run that ran an item to a verdict decides, whether that run came from the editor, the terminal or a replay. A run that stopped before it reached an item leaves the verdict as it was.\n\nEach item in items has these fields:\n\nField Meaning\npackage The name of the package of the item. With name, its key, which identifies an item everywhere in the protocol.\nname Unique in its package.\nfile, line The absolute path, and the line of the @testitem.\nend_line The line before the next item in the file, or the last line of the file. These are the lines where the item is the one at the cursor.\ntags, setups Strings.\nprofile The profile from profile = :name, or null for the default profile.\nsandbox true for an item with sandbox = true, which runs alone in a process of its own.\nenvironment The absolute path of the environment directory of the item's file, or null for the test environment.\nchain The name of the chain, or null.\nskip true, false, or the text of the expression that the worker evaluates.\ntimeout, retries, failfast The value of the item, or null for the value of the run.\n\nerrors holds every problem that a run refuses to start on. Examples are a file that does not parse, a name that two items use, and a value that TestItems.toml does not accept. For serve_mono, it also holds what stops a combined run: two setups of one name, and an import that the test environment of its own package cannot load. Each error has a file, a line (0 when there is none) and a message. The items still come from every file that parses.","title":"items"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#run_started","page":"Editor protocol","text":"Field Meaning\nitems The keys of the items that will run, in the order of the plan.\nworkers The number of workers, or 0 for a run in the process of the server.\nseed The seed of the run, as \"0x…\" text.\nunknown The keys of the command that the suite does not have.","title":"run_started"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#item_started","page":"Editor protocol","text":"Field Meaning\npackage, name, file, line The item.\nattempt The number of the attempt, from 1.\nattempts The most attempts that the item gets.\nworker The worker slot, from 1, or 0 for an outcome recorded before a worker ran the item, such as a broken chain.\npid The ID of the process of the worker, or 0 when the worker did not start.","title":"item_started"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#item_finished","page":"Editor protocol","text":"It has the fields of item_started, and these:\n\nField Meaning\nstate passed, failed, errored, timedout, skipped, broken_chain or cancelled.\nelapsed, compile Seconds, as the item measured them. For an outcome that the run decided, the time that the item held its worker.\nnote What the run says about the attempt, for example that a retry follows. Often empty.\nlog The file with captured item output, or null when no nonempty log exists. With logs = \"eager\", output goes to stderr without a per-item log.\nfailures Each failed or erroring test: kind (fail or error), message as Test shows it, file and line.\n\nAn item that is retried, or that runs again with its chain, has an item_started and an item_finished for each attempt. The latest is its current state, and the last one before run_finished is its outcome.\n\nitem_finished reports the verdict before failure-artifact producers finish. Wait for run_finished before copying the complete run bundle.","title":"item_finished"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#run_finished","page":"Editor protocol","text":"Its fields are these:\n\nstate: passed, failed, cancelled, stalled or error. stalled means that the run stopped as hung. error comes after an error event that says why.\ncounts: the number of items in each state.\nnot_run: the keys of the items that never finished.\nelapsed: seconds.\nrunstate: the run state file of a run of one package, <run>.rundata/run.runstate, or null.\nrunstates: the run state file of each package, each a package and a path. A combined run writes one for each package. The path is null for a package whose run state the run failed to write.\nlogdir: the directory of the item logs. Normally this is the directory of the run, which also holds the run state, and the logs follow its retention rules. Only fallback logs in temporary storage are removed when the next run starts or the server stops.\n\nIf execution fails before a run can be initialized, run_finished has only state and the run's ID, when supplied. The other fields above are absent.","title":"run_finished"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#error","page":"Editor protocol","text":"Its fields are message, and errors when a run did not start because the suite does not parse. errors has the same form as in items. The server answers each of these problems with an error, and continues:\n\na command that is not JSON, not an object, not a command, or not a command that the server knows,\na field of the wrong type,\na key in options that run does not take,\na second run while a run is in progress,\na cancel with nothing to cancel.","title":"error"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#bye","page":"Editor protocol","text":"The last line. The server writes it when stdin ends or after a shutdown command. Before it writes bye, the server cancels a run in progress and waits for the run to stop.","title":"bye"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/editor-protocol/#A-session","page":"Editor protocol","text":"→ {\"id\":1,\"command\":\"list\"}\n← {\"event\":\"items\",\"id\":1,\"root\":\"/pkg\",\"testdir\":\"/pkg/test\",\"packages\":[…],\"items\":[{\"package\":\"Pkg1\",\"name\":\"adds\",\"file\":\"/pkg/test/a_test.jl\",\"line\":1,\"end_line\":4,…}],\"errors\":[],\"failed\":[]}\n→ {\"id\":2,\"command\":\"run\",\"items\":[{\"package\":\"Pkg1\",\"name\":\"adds\"}],\"options\":{\"workers\":1}}\n← {\"event\":\"run_started\",\"id\":2,\"items\":[{\"package\":\"Pkg1\",\"name\":\"adds\"}],\"workers\":1,\"seed\":\"0x0223a77cb668dc62\",\"unknown\":[]}\n← {\"event\":\"item_started\",\"id\":2,\"package\":\"Pkg1\",\"name\":\"adds\",\"file\":\"/pkg/test/a_test.jl\",\"line\":1,\"attempt\":1,\"attempts\":1,\"worker\":1,\"pid\":4242}\n← {\"event\":\"item_finished\",\"id\":2,\"package\":\"Pkg1\",\"name\":\"adds\",…,\"state\":\"passed\",\"elapsed\":0.03,\"compile\":0.02,\"note\":\"\",\"log\":null,\"failures\":[]}\n← {\"event\":\"run_finished\",\"id\":2,\"state\":\"passed\",\"counts\":{\"passed\":1,…},\"not_run\":[],\"elapsed\":2.1,\"runstate\":\"…\",\"runstates\":[…],\"logdir\":\"…\"}\n→ {\"command\":\"shutdown\"}\n← {\"event\":\"bye\"}","title":"A session"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/files/#files","page":"Files","text":"This page describes run-state bundles and files that Runtests writes. Syntax describes the files it discovers under test/.","title":"Files"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/files/#run-state-files","page":"Files","text":"A standalone run keeps its run state and logs in a directory of its own, <run>.rundata, in the run state directory. A combined run writes one such directory for each participating package; each contains only that package's items:\n\n<run>.rundata/\n  run.runstate              # the record of the run\n  item_7_1.log\n  item_7_1.failure.txt\n  item_7_1.artifacts/\n    files/                  # Runtests.artifacts_dir()\n    producer_1/             # the files of the first producer\n    producer_2/\n  item_7_1.artifacts.log    # what the producers printed, their errors, and their times\n\nThe name <run> is the start time of the run in microseconds and the ID of its process, for example 1790247276123456-96211. If a directory with that name exists, the run adds _2, _3 and so on to the name. The names of the logs are item_<i>_<attempt>, where <i> is the number of the item and <attempt> is the attempt.\n\nFile Contents\nrun.runstate The binary record of the run. Runtests.read_run_state reads it, or the directory of the run.\nitem_<i>_<a>.log Captured output of the attempt. logs = :eager streams this output without saving a per-item log.\nitem_<i>_<a>.failure.txt The failure report of an attempt that did not pass.\nitem_<i>_<a>.artifacts/files/ The outputs that the item wrote to Runtests.artifacts_dir().\nitem_<i>_<a>.artifacts/producer_<k>/ The files that the producer k wrote.\nitem_<i>_<a>.artifacts.log What the producers printed, their errors and their times.\nsetup.log The error that stopped a run before its first item.\n\nRuntests deletes an empty log file at the end of the run. When Runtests deletes a run state, it deletes the directory of the run with it. Another editor run does not remove the logs, and neither does stopping the server. Run states and history tells which run states Runtests keeps.","title":"Run state files"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/files/#The-run-state-directory","page":"Files","text":"By default, the run state directory is runtests/runs/<project>-<hash>/ in the first Julia depot, for example ~/.julia/runtests/runs/MyPackage-8db4f545/. <project> is the name of the project, and <hash> is a hash of the path of the project. Thus each checkout has its own directory. RUNTESTS_RUNSTATE_DIR names another directory, which several projects can share. Several processes can use one run state directory at the same time. Each run makes a new directory for itself, and no two runs get the same directory. Run states and history explains which run states Runtests keeps.","title":"The run state directory"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/files/#The-artifact-directory","page":"Files","text":"When RUNTESTS_ARTIFACT_DIR is set, each run copies its directory, <run>.rundata, into that directory when it ends. It also does this after a failure or an interrupt. A run of several packages copies the directory of each package into a directory with the name of the package. The closing block names the artifact directory on the copied to line.","title":"The artifact directory"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/files/#Files-in-the-package","page":"Files","text":"Runtests also writes these files in a package or workspace:\n\nlcov.info at the package root for a standalone run, or at the workspace root for a combined run, when coverage = true. Measure coverage describes the file.\nthe expansion of each template, test/<name>_tests.jl, from Runtests.chores(),\ntest/testsetups/<Name>/Project.toml and test/testsetups/<Name>/src/<Name>.jl, from Runtests.chores() when it converts a setup into a package.\n\nRuntests.chores_mono() performs the same file-writing chores for each participating package. Commit the files that either chores command writes.","title":"Files in the package"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#running","page":"Run tests","text":"This page covers runs of one package: how to start one, select its items, and read its result. For one run across several packages in a Julia workspace, see Run the suites of a monorepo.","title":"Run tests"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Start-a-run","page":"Run tests","text":"There are two ways to start a run:\n\nCall Pkg.test() with the project of the package active. Pkg.test runs test/runtests.jl, which calls Runtests.runtests().\nCall Runtests.runtests() at the REPL with the project of the package active. Runtests must be in an environment that Julia can load from, for example your default environment.\n\nUnder Pkg.test(), Runtests uses the package whose test/runtests.jl is running. At the REPL, it uses the package of the active project. To run the suite of another package, give its directory as the first argument: Runtests.runtests(\"path/to/Package\").\n\nAt the REPL, Runtests reuses an active test project or prepares a test environment. It caches an environment it prepares for the session, reusing it while its input files remain unchanged. The test environment explains when it is rebuilt.","title":"Start a run"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Select-items","page":"Run tests","text":"With no arguments, a run selects the whole suite, or the default group when the configuration declares one. These calls select a part of the suite:\n\nRuntests.runtests(\"test/solver_test.jl\")     # one file\nRuntests.runtests(\"test/db\")                 # the files in a directory\nRuntests.runtests(\"test/solver_test.jl:42\")  # the item that contains line 42\nRuntests.runtests(name = \"adds numbers\")     # one name, exactly\nRuntests.runtests(name = r\"numbers$\")        # the names that match a Regex\nRuntests.runtests(tags = :fast)              # the items with this tag\nRuntests.runtests(tags = [:fast, :math])     # the items with both tags\nRuntests.runtests(tags = \"fast && !slow\")    # a tag expression\nRuntests.runtests(group = \"gpu\")             # a group from TestItems.toml\nRuntests.runtests(\"test/db\"; tags = :fast)   # a path and a tag together\n\nEach selection narrows the run. When you give several selections, an item runs only if all of them select it. Several paths are the exception: an item runs if any of the paths selects it. Thus a list of paths that includes the package or its test/ directory selects the whole suite.\n\nThe name keyword also takes a vector or a set of exact names. A tag expression uses !, && and ||, and && binds tighter than ||. Parentheses are not part of the syntax. The syntax reference gives the full grammar.","title":"Select items"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Select-one-item-by-its-line","page":"Run tests","text":"A target of the form file.jl:line selects the item that contains that line. The line must be in the file. A file.jl:line target is the only positional target of its call, so you cannot give other paths with it. The other selections still apply. If they do not select that item, the run selects nothing.","title":"Select one item by its line"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Select-a-group","page":"Run tests","text":"A group is a named tag expression that test/TestItems.toml declares under [groups]. Configure runs tells you how to declare groups. When a call selects nothing else, the run selects a group in this order:\n\nThe group that the environment variable RUNTESTS_GROUP names, if it is set. CI jobs use this variable to split a suite.\nThe group named default, if [groups] declares it.\nThe whole suite.\n\ngroup = \"all\" removes the group restriction; other selections still narrow the run. A path, a line, name, tags or group in the call replaces RUNTESTS_GROUP and default. Thus an item that you ask for by name runs, whatever its group.","title":"Select a group"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Selections-that-are-errors","page":"Run tests","text":"A selection that cannot mean what you intended is an error. The run does not select everything or nothing in its place. These selections are errors:\n\nan empty name or an empty tags,\na tag that no item of the suite carries,\na name that the suite does not have,\na group that [groups] does not declare,\na misspelled keyword.\n\nWhen a nearby spelling exists, the error message suggests it. For example, a misspelled group name can get the name of the group that you probably meant.\n\nThe selection narrows what runs, but not what Runtests reads. Each run parses the whole suite and makes sure that no two items have the same name, also when you select one item.","title":"Selections that are errors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Run-the-failing-items","page":"Run tests","text":"Runtests.runtestsf() runs the items whose latest verdict was not a pass. Failures include failed tests, errors, timeouts, a chain cut short by a lost worker, and an item left running when its coordinator died. Runtests keeps the latest verdict of each item across runs. Thus a run of one failure does not forget the other failures. Run states and history explains which records count.\n\nThese rules apply to runtestsf:\n\nA skip is not a verdict. A skipped item that failed before stays failing.\nA run that explicitly cancels an item, or stops before reaching it, leaves its previous verdict unchanged.\nAn item that the suite no longer has, for example because you renamed it, does not run.\nEach other selection narrows the failing items, so an item that is not failing never runs. If no failing item matches name, the call is an error.\nEach item starts from the seed that it failed with. Give seed to use another seed. Reproducing the seed does not reproduce external state or task scheduling; Investigate failures explains the limits.\n\nWhen no item is failing, runtestsf() says so and returns nothing. Thus you can call it again until the suite passes.\n\nThe retries setting controls additional attempts within one run. Configure runs explains this setting; Keep evidence from a failed attempt explains what survives when a retry passes.","title":"Run the failing items"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Inspect-the-plan","page":"Run tests","text":"Runtests.runtests(dry_run = true) prints the plan of a run and runs nothing. The plan shows each item in the order that the run is expected to start it, with the worker that is expected to run it. A dry run returns nothing. How a run works explains the order.","title":"Inspect the plan"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Run-in-this-process","page":"Run tests","text":"workers = 0 runs the items in the process that calls runtests, one after the other. An item that needs a process of its own still gets one. These are items with sandbox = true, items that run under a profile other than default, and items in an environment directory. The run starts a process for each such item or chain, stops it afterwards, and lists these items. A chain shares that process through its members and retries unless the process is lost.\n\nItems that run in the caller's process, their profile hooks and their artifact producers have no enforceable timeout: there is no separate process to stop. Items that get a worker keep their timeouts. Coverage needs workers, so workers = 0 with coverage = true is an error.","title":"Run in this process"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#What-a-run-returns","page":"Run tests","text":"A normal run returns a Runtests.RunTestSet. A RunTestSet is a testset that holds one testset for each test file. A dry run returns nothing.\n\nThe result of a failure depends on where you call runtests:\n\nOutside a @testset, an item that did not pass makes the call throw. This is how Pkg.test knows that the tests failed.\nInside a @testset, the run records its results in that testset. Several runs inside one @testset add up there. Give each run a different testset_name to tell them apart.\n\nA process runs one run at a time. A call to runtests while another run is in progress, for example from another task, throws an error. The first run continues.","title":"What a run returns"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Errors-before-a-run-starts","page":"Run tests","text":"Some problems stop the run before any item runs. These problems throw an exception:\n\nException Cause\nRuntests.ScanFailure A test file that does not parse, a name that two items use, a Julia file under test/ that is not a test file, or a template expansion that is missing or not current.\nRuntests.NoTestsError No test files, no item that the selection matches, or existing failing items of which none matches the selection. With no failing items, runtestsf() returns nothing.\nRuntests.ConfigError A mistake in test/TestItems.toml, in another configuration file or in an environment variable. Examples are an incorrect value, an unknown profile, an undeclared group in RUNTESTS_GROUP, or a profile environment that does not resolve.\nArgumentError A mistake in the call. Examples are a misspelled keyword, a value out of range, an empty selection, a malformed tag expression, or a path outside test/.\n\nA ScanFailure lists every problem that Runtests found, so you can correct them all before the next run.","title":"Errors before a run starts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/chores/#chores","page":"Maintain the suite","text":"Runtests.chores() does the maintenance work of a suite. It does the work that needs no person, then reports what does need a person. Run it after you add a setup, edit a template or change test/TestItems.toml.\n\nFor all participating packages of a workspace in one pass, use Runtests.chores_mono().\n\nRuntests.chores(dry_run = true)  # report what to change, and change nothing\nRuntests.chores()                # do the changes, and report what remains","title":"Maintain the suite"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/chores/#What-chores-does","page":"Maintain the suite","text":"chores does these steps in order:\n\nIt converts the setups into packages, and adds the packages that they started to import.\nIt expands the templates, and deletes the expansions whose templates are gone.\nIt reads the test items and test/TestItems.toml, and reports each problem that stops a run. Examples are a syntax error, a name that two items use and an incorrect value. Other examples are an [order] entry that names no item, an unknown profile, and a group with a tag that no item carries.\nIt deletes the run states that a run deletes when it prunes, by the same retention rules, together with the directories of their runs.\nIf the project keeps its run states in the default location, it deletes the run states of checkouts that no longer exist, with their directories.\n\nchores never changes a test item, a configuration file or an expansion that a person edited. It never deletes a run state of another machine, for example of CI, or a run state that this version of Runtests cannot read.\n\nA package without test files has nothing to read in step 3. An example is a fixture package in the test/testfixtures/ of another package.","title":"What chores does"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/chores/#What-chores-returns","page":"Maintain the suite","text":"chores() returns true when nothing remains. When a problem remains that a person must correct, it throws Runtests.ChoresError, after it did the rest of its work. Thus this line stops before any test runs on a suite that needs a person first:\n\nRuntests.chores(); Runtests.runtests()\n\nchores(dry_run = true) changes nothing. It returns true only when nothing needs work, neither by a person nor by chores. Reported maintenance problems return false without throwing ChoresError; invalid arguments or paths and unexpected I/O errors can still throw.","title":"What chores returns"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/chores/#Read-other-configuration-files","page":"Maintain the suite","text":"A run can read another configuration file in place of test/TestItems.toml, with the config keyword. To make sure that such files are valid too, give them to chores:\n\nRuntests.chores(config = \"ci.toml\")\nRuntests.chores(config = [\"ci.toml\", \"nightly.toml\"])\n\nchores reads each file in the same way as a run does, and also reads test/TestItems.toml. A relative path is relative to the current directory.","title":"Read other configuration files"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/chores/#Progress","page":"Maintain the suite","text":"chores prints a block with one line for each step. A step that takes longer than 3 seconds shows its duration. While chores expands templates, it prints the progress of each template. For example, it prints the time to load the packages that the template imports.\n\n┌ [TEST] chores\n│ setups: 1 of 1 changed:\n│   `ShapeData`: moved to ShapeData/src/ShapeData.jl · deps: none\n│ templates: none\n│ test items: 3 in 1 file, all valid\n│ config: none\n│ run states: 5 in ~/.julia/runtests/runs/Geometry-…, none to delete\n│ checkouts that are gone: none with run states to delete\n└ done: 1\n\nThe first argument finds the package as it does for runtests. Without it, chores uses the package of the active project.","title":"Progress"}]
}
